{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ba4f3e70206384eb", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tHybridSB-MoE: Dual-Domain Schrödinger Bridges\n2\twith Scene-Adaptive Expert Routing for Speech\n3\tEnhancement\n4\t\n5\tarXiv:2608.12715v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n6\t\n7\tZhengyi Lu Aswini Sivakumar Jie Hu Yao Qiang\n8\tDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering, Oakland University\n9\tRochester, MI 48309\n10\t{zhengyilu, aswinisivakumar, jiehu, qiang}@oakland.edu\n11\t\n12\tAbstract\n13\tGenerative speech enhancement faces three gaps: spectral models capture harmonic\n14\tstructure but often disrupt phase, waveform models preserve phase but miss harmonics, and Schrödinger Bridges (SB) shorten transport from noise to clean speech\n15\tbut leave inference cost only loosely tied to training. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\n16\ta dual-domain framework that fills these gaps through three contributions unified\n17\tby a single asymmetric design principle. (i) Asymmetric uncertainty fusion: The\n18\tspectral path captures epistemic uncertainty via expert disagreement, while the\n19\twaveform bridge models aleatoric variance through stochastic dynamics. We fuse\n20\tthem asymmetrically, allowing the mixing weight to adapt to distinct error regimes\n21\trather than average predictions. (ii) Heterogeneous MoE with top-k=2 routing\n22\tacross five distinct architectural archetypes, where architectural diversity makes the\n23\tepistemic signal indicate which inductive bias fails rather than small perturbations\n24\tamong similar experts. (iii) Discretization bound (Theorem 1): path-consistency\n25\tand trajectory regularizers together bound the K-step bridge sampling error in\n26\t2-Wasserstein distance at rate K −α , making small-K inference an objective-level\n27\tguarantee rather than an empirical claim. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, HybridSBMoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at their step budgets while\n28\tremaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods.\n29\t\n30\t1\n31\t\n32\tIntroduction\n33\t\n34\tSpeech enhancement (SE) has long faced a structural domain trade-off. Spectral methods are\n35\teffective at modeling harmonic structure and stationary noise, but often fragment phase across STFT\n36\tframes; waveform methods preserve phase continuity, but can struggle with harmonically structured\n37\tinterference (Fig. 1). This trade-off becomes especially limiting in real-world deployment, where\n38\ta hearing aid on a moving bus, a video-conferencing system in a crowded café, or an in-car voice\n39\tassistant must handle harmonic engine drone, broadband ambient hum, and non-stationary crowd\n40\tbabble under millisecond latency budgets and without scene labels at inference time [1, 2]. These\n41\tsettings require more than a universally stronger model: they require a mechanism that can exploit\n42\tcomplementary inductive biases and decide which one to trust for each input.\n43\tA decade of deep learning has advanced SE from DNN masking [3, 4], through time-domain\n44\t[5, 2, 6] and spectro-temporal [7, 8] architectures to diffusion-based generative models [9, 10, 11] and\n45\tSchrödinger Bridge formulations that replace the uninformative Gaussian prior of diffusion with direct\n46\ttransport from noisy to clean speech. Despite this progress, three structural limitations persist that are\n47\tprecisely what a dual-domain framework must address. (i) Single-domain commitment. Existing\n48\tmethods typically operate in either the waveform or spectral domain, sacrificing the complementary\n49\tinductive bias of the other [8]. (ii) Uniform processing across heterogeneous noise. A single\n50\tPreprint.\n51\t\n52\t\fnetwork handles stationary appliance hum, harmonic engine noise, and non-stationary crowd babble\n53\talike, despite their structurally different inductive biases [12, 13]. (iii) Loosely controlled sampling\n54\tcost. Generative SE pipelines often require many iterative refinement steps, with limited formal\n55\tconnection between the training objective and the inference budget [10, 11].\n56\tThe natural response, i.e., running a spectral and a waveform pathway in parallel and combining\n57\tthem, fails on its own, because a generic ensemble of two prediction streams provides no mechanism\n58\tto know which pathway is failing on a given input. Without such signal, the combination defaults\n59\tto fixed-weight averaging, which is provably suboptimal whenever the two branches’ errors are\n60\tuncorrelated, and the dual-domain promise reduces to a parameter-count increase.\n61\tWe introduce HybridSB-MoE, a dual-domain framework organized around one asymmetric idea: a multiexpert spectral path naturally produces an epistemic uncertainty signal (which expert is right?), and a stochastic\n62\twaveform bridge naturally produces an aleatoric one\n63\t(intrinsic transport noise). These are categorically different signals about categorically different errors. Pairing\n64\tthe two pathways and fusing on this asymmetry yields\n65\ta mixing weight that selects between two error regimes\n66\trather than averaging two predictions, transforming the\n67\tdual-domain promise into a principled co-design. Two\n68\tfurther architectural choices are required for this idea to\n69\twork. First, the spectral pathway must produce informative expert disagreement, which requires architectural Figure 1: The persistent dual-domain diheterogeneity rather than capacity-replicated variation. chotomy in speech enhancement. WaveWe therefore use heterogeneous expert archetypes, each form processing preserves temporal fine\n70\taligned with a canonical noise-processing primitive, in- structure and phase coherence but undercluding low-rank denoising, wide receptive fields, in- performs on harmonically structured information bottlenecks, harmonic bases, and universal terference; spectral processing captures\n71\tapproximation, and combine them under sparse routing. harmonic structure and stationary noise\n72\tSecond, the waveform pathway must remain faithful un- patterns but fragments phase across STFT\n73\tder the small inference budget K targeted by the system. frames. The two domains succeed and fail\n74\tWe therefore train it with path-consistency and trajectory in opposite regimes, and existing generaregularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that jointly min- tive methods inherit one half of the tradeimizing them bounds the K-step bridge discretization off by committing to a single domain.\n75\terror in 2-Wasserstein distance, making small-K inference a consequence of the training objective\n76\trather than an empirical heuristic. In Section 3, we further show that no proper subset of these three\n77\tcomponents recovers the central design property.\n78\tOur contributions are as follows:\n79\t1. A dual-domain framework built around asymmetric uncertainty. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\n80\ta dual-domain framework that couples spectral MoE and waveform SB pathways. By pairing each\n81\tpath with its natural uncertainty signal, epistemic disagreement for spectral experts and aleatoric\n82\tvariance for the waveform bridge, HybridSB-MoE enables fusion that adapts to error regimes rather\n83\tthan fixed-weight averaging.\n84\t2. A heterogeneous spectral MoE for informative epistemic disagreement. We design a sparse\n85\ttop-k=2 spectral MoE with architecturally distinct expert archetypes. This heterogeneity makes\n86\texpert disagreement reflect mismatched inductive biases rather than small perturbations among\n87\tcapacity-replicated experts.\n88\t3. A regularized waveform bridge for few-step inference. We train the waveform SB with\n89\tpath consistency and trajectory regularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that they bound the K-step\n90\tdiscretization error in 2-Wasserstein distance, linking small-K inference to the training objective.\n91\t4. SOTA on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE improves over diffusion- and SB-based\n92\tbaselines at their respective step budgets, remains competitive with consistency-distilled few-step\n93\tmethods, and provides calibrated fusion uncertainty for input-adaptive dual-domain enhancement.\n94\t\n95\t2\n96\t\n97\t\f2\n98\t\n99\tRelated Work\n100\t\n101\tDiscriminative SE. The field has progressed from DNN masking [3, 4] that surpassed classical\n102\tWiener and MMSE estimators [14, 15], through time-domain encoder–decoders (Conv-TasNet [5, 16],\n103\tDEMUCS [2]), dual-path sequence models [6], full-sub-band hybrids (FullSubNet+ [7]), spectrotemporal grids (TF-GridNet [8]), and recent Mamba-based linear-complexity backbones [17, 18].\n104\tJoint enhancement objectives that combine SI-SDR with perceptual surrogates push fidelity further\n105\t[19, 20], yet the underlying paradigm remains a one-shot deterministic regression. Two limitations\n106\ttherefore persist regardless of architecture. (i) Inherent ambiguity: under severe non-stationary\n107\tor harmonically structured noise, the noisy-to-clean mapping admits multiple plausible solutions,\n108\tand a deterministic regressor must collapse this multimodal posterior to a single point estimate,\n109\twhich typically manifests as residual noise or over-suppressed harmonics [21]. (ii) No uncertainty:\n110\tdiscriminative networks expose no signal at inference time to flag this ambiguity, so a downstream\n111\tmodule cannot tell when the prediction should be trusted—making them unsuitable as one branch of\n112\ta confidence-routed dual-domain combiner.\n113\tGenerative models and Schrödinger Bridges for SE. Likelihood-based [21] and score-based\n114\t[9, 22, 23] diffusion models reverse a noise corruption process; SGMSE/SGMSE+ [10, 11] apply\n115\tthis in the complex spectrogram domain, jointly modeling magnitude and phase, but require many\n116\titerative steps from an uninformative Gaussian prior. Two recent paradigms exploit the structure of\n117\tthe noisy observation directly. Flow-matching approaches [24, 25] regress velocity fields between\n118\tcoupled distributions, shortening the sampling trajectory but typically without an explicit stochastic\n119\tperturbation. Schrödinger Bridges [26, 27] learn the optimal-transport-like coupling between the\n120\tnoisy observation and clean speech, retaining a small bridge variance for full-support marginals.\n121\tSB for SE [28, 29, 30, 31, 32] confirm improved low-SNR structure preservation along this shorter\n122\tpath. Empirical step-count reductions have been achieved by consistency distillation [33, 34],\n123\tadversarial few-step training [35], and SB-consistency trajectory models [36]. None of these works\n124\tlinks the training objective to the inference budget, where small K is asserted but not derived.\n125\tTheoretical convergence analyses for diffusion sampling exist in the broader literature [37], but their\n126\tbounds are stated in terms of the score-matching error rather than tractable, training-time-minimized\n127\tregularizers, and have not been adapted to the doubly-conditioned SB setting. Moreover, these\n128\tmethods remain single-domain and lack calibrated uncertainty for fusion. We address both with\n129\tasymmetric uncertainty fusion and a discretization bound (Theorem 1) that links small-K inference\n130\tto explicit training-time quantities.\n131\tMixture-of-Experts for SE. MoE enables conditional computation with sparse routing, scaling\n132\tcapacity without proportional inference cost [38, 39, 40]. In SE, sparse MoE [12] first enabled\n133\tscene-adaptive processing via noise-clustered experts; zero-shot personalization [41] extended this to\n134\tspeakers via embedding-based routing; clean-cluster pre-training [13] enforced expert specialization\n135\tthrough pre-training initialization; and dynamic capacity allocation [42] improved routing efficiency\n136\tat inference. However, this line of work shares three limitations. (i) Homogeneity. Existing methods\n137\treplicate a single architecture across experts, assuming one computational primitive can handle tonal,\n138\tambient, harmonic, and crowd noise. This assumption breaks down under realistic, structurally\n139\tdiverse noise. (ii) Routing without uncertainty. Existing routing only partitions inputs into specialist\n140\tregions, rather than exposing interpretable uncertainty; thus, the epistemic signal from multi-expert\n141\tdisagreement remains unused. (iii) Separation from generative SE. MoE routing and generative bridge\n142\tmodels remain disconnected in SE, leaving scene-adaptive computation and structured distributional\n143\ttransport as separate lines of work. We address all three with heterogeneous archetype experts that\n144\tencode distinct noise-specific inductive biases. Their disagreement provides the epistemic signal\n145\twhich, together with the SB’s aleatoric variance, drives asymmetric fusion. To our knowledge, this is\n146\tthe first joint MoE–SB framework for SE.\n147\tUncertainty quantification and dual-branch fusion. Calibrated uncertainty for deep regression is\n148\tmost commonly obtained via deep ensembles [43] or learned heteroscedastic variance heads, with\n149\tclassification calibration assessed via ECE [44]. Prior dual-branch SE methods that combine timeand frequency-domain estimates typically use either fixed-weight averaging or learned attention over\n150\tpredictions [8, 2]; none assigns categorically distinct uncertainty types to the two branches, and so the\n151\tfusion cannot route on which kind of error each branch is exposed to. Our pathway-typed asymmetric\n152\tfusion is, to our knowledge, the first to exploit this structure, and the calibration loss (Eq. (11)) ties\n153\tthe abstract epistemic/aleatoric labels to measurable per-pathway reconstruction errors so the routing\n154\tweight is learned, not asserted.\n155\t3\n156\t\n157\t\fFigure 2: Overview of HybridSB-MoE. The spectral pathway (top) routes log-magnitude features\n158\tz = log |S{y}| through N heterogeneous archetype experts via gating G(z) to produce xspec (t).\n159\tThe waveform pathway (bottom) iteratively refines an SB state [xt , y] through a U-Net to produce\n160\txwave (t). An uncertainty-aware fusion combines both via uepi (top-k expert disagreement) and uale\n161\t(bridge-variance head) to yield x̂(t).\n162\t\n163\t3\n164\t\n165\tMethod\n166\t\n167\tHybridSB-MoE has three coupled components: a heterogeneous spectral MoE, an SB waveform\n168\tpathway with path-consistency and trajectory regularizers (for which we prove a K-step discretization\n169\tbound, Theorem 1), and an asymmetric uncertainty fusion that selects between epistemic and aleatoric\n170\terror regimes. Figure 2 illustrates the overall HybridSB-MoE framework. We develop each component\n171\tin turn and close the section by showing that no proper subset recovers the central design property.\n172\tOverview. Given a noisy input y(t), HybridSB-MoE processes it through two parallel pathways\n173\tdesigned to capture complementary speech characteristics. The spectral pathway applies the heterogeneous MoE to the log-magnitude STFT for scene-adaptive enhancement. In parallel, the waveform\n174\tpathway runs an efficient SB on the time-domain signal to preserve phase. The two pathways exhibit\n175\tasymmetric reliability across acoustic conditions: spectral processing excels in harmonically structured noise, whereas waveform processing is more robust in phase-sensitive scenarios. Consequently,\n176\ta fixed fusion strategy is inherently suboptimal. We therefore introduce an uncertainty-aware fusion\n177\tmodule that integrates xspec (t) and xwave (t) by dynamically weighting each pathway based on\n178\tconfidence, producing the final enhanced signal x̂(t). Both pathways run in parallel at inference, and\n179\tlong utterances use overlap-add segmentation (Appendix B).\n180\tSpectral feature extraction. We apply the STFT [45] to obtain the complex spectrogram S{y} ∈\n181\tCF ×Tf (F frequency bins) and decompose it in polar form into magnitude |S{y}| and phase ∠S{y},\n182\tprocessed by separate heads (below). The MoE consumes log-magnitude features z = log |S{y}| ∈\n183\tRF ×Tf , whose logarithmic scaling compresses the dynamic range in line with human hearing.\n184\tHeterogeneous mixture-of-experts. Unlike conventional homogeneous MoE designs [38, 39, 40] in\n185\twhich all experts share the same architecture and differ only in learned weights, we instantiate distinct\n186\tarchitectural archetypes, each tailored to a canonical noise-processing primitive, i.e., Home, Nature,\n187\tOffice, Transport, and Public for VoiceBank+DEMAND (see Appendix A for full specification).\n188\tUnder sparse top-k routing, pairwise mixtures and weighted blends of these archetypes cover the 14\n189\tnoise categories in VoiceBank+DEMAND [46] combinatorially rather than by a dedicated expert\n190\tper noise, and the design extends to new acoustic environments by adding archetypes per noise\n191\tfamily encountered, without altering the rest of the framework (see Appendix A for details). All\n192\texperts share a backbone of normalization (group norm [47]) → variable internal modules →\n193\tbottleneck projection, ensuring routing-interface compatibility while permitting structurally divergent\n194\tintermediate computation. This design, a small semantically anchored basis composed of sparse\n195\t4\n196\t\n197\t\frouting, replaces the parameter scaling of large homogeneous MoEs with interpretable routing and\n198\tmeaningful expert disagreement, which we use for asymmetric fusion below.\n199\tScene-adaptive sparse routing. Standard sparse routing [38, 39] assigns each input token to its\n200\ttop-k experts independently, which is well-suited to language modelling but ill-suited to SE: the\n201\tdominant noise type is a global property of an utterance, while local acoustic events (a phoneme onset,\n202\ta transient hit) require frame-level adaptivity. We therefore use a two-level gate: Archetype-level\n203\trouting Garch (z) pools z across time and decides the dominant archetype mixture for the whole\n204\tutterance; token-level routing Gtoken (z) refines this assignment per frame. The two combine as\n205\tG(z) = αGarch (z) + (1 − α)Gtoken (z) with α ∈ [0, 1]. An MLP maps G(z) to per-expert logits;\n206\tthe top-k entries are softmax-renormalized to weights {Gi (z)}i∈Ik , and the routed output is\n207\tX\n208\tx̂spec =\n209\tGi (z) Ei (z),\n210\t(1)\n211\ti∈Ik\n212\t\n213\twhere Ik indexes the selected experts and Ei are expert networks. To prevent expert collapse [38, 39],\n214\ta standard load-balancing regularizer\n215\tLaux = λI Var(pi ) + λL Var(ni )\n216\t\n217\t(2)\n218\t\n219\tpenalizes uneven utilization, where pi and ni denote the average routing probability and token count\n220\tof expert i, respectively.\n221\tSpectral reconstruction. The routed features x̂spec pass through two parallel 1 × 1-conv heads.\n222\tSince phase cannot be inferred from log-magnitude alone, the phase head also consumes the noisy\n223\tphase as (sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y}), concatenated along the channel dimension:\n224\tM̂ = Mmax sigmoid(hmag (x̂spec )), ∆ϕ = ϕmax tanh(hpha (x̂spec , sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y})), (3)\n225\twhere Mmax , ϕmax bound the mask and phase correction to prevent over-suppression, excessive\n226\tamplification, and phase-wrap instability. The enhanced spectrum Ŝ = M̂ ⊙ |S{y}| · ej(∠S{y}+∆ϕ)\n227\tis inverted to xspec (t) via iSTFT with overlap-add. STFT processing nevertheless fragments temporal\n228\tfine structure through frame-wise phase decoupling, motivating the parallel waveform pathway below.\n229\tSchrödinger Bridge formulation. The continuous-time SB problem seeks the stochastic process\n230\tminimizing the KL divergence to a reference Brownian motion subject to fixed marginal distributions\n231\tat both endpoints [26, 27, 48]. In our application the two endpoints are the noisy observation y\n232\tand the clean target x, so the bridge directly couples the conditional distributions our system must\n233\ttransport between. Where standard diffusion [9] traverses the full path from a data-independent\n234\tGaussian prior, the bridge exploits the substantial mutual information between y and x to yield a\n235\tshorter, more informative trajectory and, empirically, fewer sampling steps and better low-SNR\n236\tstructure preservation [28, 29, 30]. Conceptually adjacent flow-matching methods [24, 25] also\n237\tcouple paired distributions but typically without an explicit bridge perturbation; our SB construction\n238\tretains a small noise term σt ϵ to keep the marginal p(xt | x, y) full-support, which is required for\n239\tscore-based reverse sampling and for the trajectory regularizer to act as a self-consistency constraint.\n240\tThe intermediate state at diffusion time t ∈ [0, T ] interpolates between y and x with a small Gaussian\n241\tperturbation,\n242\tq\n243\tq\n244\txt =\n245\t\n246\tβ̄t x +\n247\t\n248\t1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ,\n249\t\n250\tϵ ∼ N (0, I),\n251\t\n252\t(4)\n253\t\n254\twhere β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +s)/(1+s)·π/2) is a cumulative cosine p\n255\tschedule [49]\n256\tdecreasing monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0 (offset s=0.008), and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n257\t(σmax =0.05) vanishes at both endpoints, so x0 = x and xT = y deterministically while σt ϵ supplies\n258\tthe full-support marginal p(xt | x, y) required by the score-based reverse process.\n259\tThe reverse process is parameterized as a learnable denoiser x̂θ (xt , y, t) that predicts the clean target\n260\tdirectly from the bridge state and the observation. Rather than solving the reverse-time stochastic\n261\tdifferential equation (SDE) associated with the bridge in closed form, we adopt a tractable bridgeconsistent update that iteratively re-injects the predicted clean estimate into the forward construction\n262\tof Eq. (4) at the previous timestep:\n263\tq\n264\tq\n265\txt−1 = β̄t−1 x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t−1 y + σt−1 z,\n266\tz ∼ N (0, I).\n267\t(5)\n268\t5\n269\t\n270\t\fThis data-prediction update is the bridge analogue of DDPM/DDIM ancestral sampling [9, 23]: deterministic when σt−1 =0, stochastic otherwise. Bridge-marginal consistency is enforced empirically\n271\tthrough the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced below. The denoiser is a 1D\n272\tU-Net with transformer bottleneck and FiLM timestep conditioning (Appendix B). The training\n273\tobjective is the data-prediction loss:\n274\t2\n275\t\n276\tLdata\n277\tSB = Et,(x,y),ϵ x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x 2 ,\n278\t\n279\t(6)\n280\t\n281\taugmented by the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced next.\n282\tPath-consistency and trajectory regularizers. A fixed front-loaded schedule tk = T (k/K)γ with\n283\tγ<1 concentrates the K-step budget in the early-trajectory regime where the SNR changes fastest and\n284\twhere errors propagate to all later steps [9]. Two regularizers then encourage a smooth, low-curvature\n285\tdenoising path in the (y, x) subspace. The path-consistency loss enforces cross-timestep agreement\n286\tof clean-signal predictions along the same trajectory:\n287\th\n288\ti\n289\t2\n290\tLpath = Et,t′ ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥\n291\t(7)\n292\twhere t, t′ are independently sampled along the same bridge trajectory. This penalizes inconsistent\n293\tclean-signal predictions across timesteps, reducing reverse-process curvature (cf. consistency models\n294\t[34], but here for path smoothing, not distillation). The trajectory loss anchors xt to the scheduleconsistent reconstruction:\n295\t\"\n296\t#\n297\tq\n298\tq\n299\t\u0001 2\n300\tLtraj = Et xt −\n301\tβ̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y\n302\t.\n303\t(8)\n304\tAt the optimum x̂θ = x, the residual reduces to σt ϵ, so Ltraj couples the prediction to the specific\n305\tbridge state xt at each timestep, complementing the in-expectation regression of Eq. (6). At inference,\n306\twe use K=8 steps with the front-loaded non-uniform discretization tk = T (k/K)γ , γ=0.6 (see\n307\tAppendix B for more details).\n308\tDiscretization bound. The two regularizers above make small-K inference viable, and we now show\n309\twhy. Let p̂K be the law of the K-step rollout of Eq. (5) at t=0 and pbr\n310\t0 the continuous-time bridge\n311\tmarginal at t=0, both conditional on y.\n312\tAssumption 1 (Regularity).\n313\tp (i) x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and Lt -Lipschitz in\n314\tt. (ii) β̄t and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) are C 1 on [0, T ], with β̄t monotone and bounded second\n315\tderivative. (iii) E∥xt ∥2 and E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 are uniformly bounded in t.\n316\tTheorem 1 (K-step bridge discretization bound). Under Assumption 1, with the schedule tk =\n317\tT (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\n318\tq\n319\t\u0001\n320\t−α\n321\tW2 p̂K , pbr\n322\t≤\n323\tC\n324\tK\n325\t+\n326\tC\n327\tL⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n328\t(9)\n329\t1\n330\t2\n331\t0\n332\twith α = min(1, γ), L⋆ the training-termination values of Eq. (7)–(8), and C1 , C2 depending only\n333\ton Lx , Lt , σmax , and the curvature of β̄t .\n334\tWith α = min(1, γ), front-loading (γ < 1) concentrates steps near t=0 where Φ′ is largest, which\n335\tis what drives small-K behavior empirically.\n336\tEach term corresponds to a separate lever: C1 K −α is\n337\t√\n338\tcontrolled by inference-time K, γ; C2 L⋆ is controlled by training-time minimization of the two\n339\t⋆\n340\tregularizers and is independent of K.\n341\t√ Two consequences follow. First, once L is small, the bound\n342\t−α\n343\tsaturates: any K with C1 K ≲ C2 L⋆ already attains the asymptotic floor, which is what justifies\n344\tour K=8 rather than the much larger K of standard diffusion sampling. Second, the regularizers\n345\tare not auxiliary terms; without them, the second term has no training-time upper bound, and the\n346\tinequality does not exist as a function of K. Therefore, removing either breaks the entire argument.\n347\tThis is the formal sense in which path-consistency and trajectory regularization are load-bearing\n348\tfor our inference schedule. Eq. (9) is closely related to recent convergence results for diffusion\n349\tsampling [37] but adapts those analyses to the doubly-conditioned bridge process and, importantly,\n350\treplaces score-matching error with the explicit, training-time-minimized regularizer pair, which\n351\tmakes the bound directly actionable as a design tool. The full proof includes a one-step fidelity\n352\tlemma combined with a non-uniform Riemann estimate under a synchronous coupling, with details\n353\tdeferred to Appendix C. We present Eq. (9) as a design-justifying inequality, not a tight predictor.\n354\t6\n355\t\n356\t\fAsymmetric uncertainty fusion. A naive dual-branch ensembler treats the two pathways symmetrically; ours does not, by design. Epistemic uncertainty (‘which expert is right?’) is well-defined only\n357\twhen multiple experts coexist (the spectral MoE); the waveform pathway has no analog. Aleatoric\n358\tuncertainty (intrinsic stochasticity of the bridge SDE in Eq. (4)) is well-defined only when the\n359\tforward process is genuinely stochastic (the waveform pathway); the deterministic spectral mask in\n360\tEq. (3) has no analog. Each pathway therefore carries exactly one uncertainty type: uepi flags an\n361\tarchitectural mismatch, uale flags a stochastic transport error. The fusion selects\n362\tP between these two\n363\terror regimes; it does not average two predictions. Concretely, uepi = kT1 f i∈Ik ∥Ei (z)− Ē(z)∥22\n364\tP\n365\twith Ē(z) = k1 i∈Ik Ei (z) (deep-ensembles sense [43], not Bayesian posterior); uale is from the\n366\tU-Net’s per-sample log-variance head, exponentiated and time-averaged. After z-score normalization,\n367\ta 2-layer MLP gives w = σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale )) ∈ [0, 1], and\n368\tx̂(t) = w · xspec (t) + (1 − w) · xwave (t).\n369\t\n370\t(10)\n371\t\n372\tHigh expert disagreement pushes w toward the waveform pathway and high SB variance toward the\n373\tspectral pathway: each pathway is deferred away from when its native error is large. A calibration\n374\tloss anchors the two scalars to the errors they should track,\n375\tLcal = (uepi − ∥xspec − x∥22 )2 + (uale − ∥xwave − x∥22 )2 ,\n376\t\n377\t(11)\n378\t\n379\twhere uepi , uale are pre-normalized; without it, the scalars have no incentive to scale with error, and\n380\tthe fusion’s dependence on them would be arbitrary.\n381\tTraining. All components are trained end-to-end. Each step runs both pathways in parallel: the\n382\tspectral path produces xspec , uepi ; the waveform path samples t, ϵ, forms xt via Eq. (4), and outputs\n383\txwave , uale . The fusion yields x̂. The objective combines reconstruction, SB, load-balancing, and\n384\tcalibration losses:\n385\tL = Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal ,\n386\t(12)\n387\t2\n388\tdata\n389\twith Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥2 and LSB = LSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj . Loss weights (gridsearched on a 10% VB split): λSB =1.0, λpath =0.1, λtraj =0.05, λaux =0.01, λcal =0.05; ceilings\n390\tMmax =5.0, ϕmax =π/4. Full procedure in Appendix B.\n391\tWhy the components are coupled, not stacked. The central design property—a fusion that routes\n392\ton epistemic vs. aleatoric error regimes at a sampling budget derived from training—requires all\n393\tthree components and survives no proper subset. Three counterfactuals make this concrete. (C1)\n394\tSymmetric uncertainty heads on both pathways (the standard dual-branch ensemble): the fusion sees\n395\ttwo aleatoric scalars and no epistemic one, making it blind to spectral expert disagreement. Thus,\n396\tthe mixing weight collapses into a generic confidence average, consistent with the 0.17 PESQ drop\n397\tobserved for “w/o uncertainty fusion” in Figure 3a. (C2) Homogeneous MoE in place of distinct\n398\tarchetypes: top-k blends span one function class instead of distinct inductive biases. In this way,\n399\tuepi no longer indicates which bias is mismatched but only weight perturbations among identicalarchitecture experts, resulting in 0.43 PESQ drop as shown in Figure 3a. (C3) Drop the regularizers:\n400\tTheorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e71df7e2-cb51-4e95-971f-0cc9eee0f3dc", "uuid": "257a55d6-8c46-4f3c-862a-00f49208f7e5", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T06:11:22.101Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "HybridSB-MoE: Dual-Domain Schrödinger Bridges\nwith Scene-Adaptive Expert Routing for Speech\nEnhancement\n\narXiv:2608.12715v1 [cs.SD] 13 Aug 2026\n\nZhengyi Lu Aswini Sivakumar Jie Hu Yao Qiang\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering, Oakland University\nRochester, MI 48309\n{zhengyilu, aswinisivakumar, jiehu, qiang}@oakland.edu\n\nAbstract\nGenerative speech enhancement faces three gaps: spectral models capture harmonic\nstructure but often disrupt phase, waveform models preserve phase but miss harmonics, and Schrödinger Bridges (SB) shorten transport from noise to clean speech\nbut leave inference cost only loosely tied to training. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\na dual-domain framework that fills these gaps through three contributions unified\nby a single asymmetric design principle. (i) Asymmetric uncertainty fusion: The\nspectral path captures epistemic uncertainty via expert disagreement, while the\nwaveform bridge models aleatoric variance through stochastic dynamics. We fuse\nthem asymmetrically, allowing the mixing weight to adapt to distinct error regimes\nrather than average predictions. (ii) Heterogeneous MoE with top-k=2 routing\nacross five distinct architectural archetypes, where architectural diversity makes the\nepistemic signal indicate which inductive bias fails rather than small perturbations\namong similar experts. (iii) Discretization bound (Theorem 1): path-consistency\nand trajectory regularizers together bound the K-step bridge sampling error in\n2-Wasserstein distance at rate K −α , making small-K inference an objective-level\nguarantee rather than an empirical claim. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, HybridSBMoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at their step budgets while\nremaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nSpeech enhancement (SE) has long faced a structural domain trade-off. Spectral methods are\neffective at modeling harmonic structure and stationary noise, but often fragment phase across STFT\nframes; waveform methods preserve phase continuity, but can struggle with harmonically structured\ninterference (Fig. 1). This trade-off becomes especially limiting in real-world deployment, where\na hearing aid on a moving bus, a video-conferencing system in a crowded café, or an in-car voice\nassistant must handle harmonic engine drone, broadband ambient hum, and non-stationary crowd\nbabble under millisecond latency budgets and without scene labels at inference time [1, 2]. These\nsettings require more than a universally stronger model: they require a mechanism that can exploit\ncomplementary inductive biases and decide which one to trust for each input.\nA decade of deep learning has advanced SE from DNN masking [3, 4], through time-domain\n[5, 2, 6] and spectro-temporal [7, 8] architectures to diffusion-based generative models [9, 10, 11] and\nSchrödinger Bridge formulations that replace the uninformative Gaussian prior of diffusion with direct\ntransport from noisy to clean speech. Despite this progress, three structural limitations persist that are\nprecisely what a dual-domain framework must address. (i) Single-domain commitment. Existing\nmethods typically operate in either the waveform or spectral domain, sacrificing the complementary\ninductive bias of the other [8]. (ii) Uniform processing across heterogeneous noise. A single\nPreprint.\n\n\fnetwork handles stationary appliance hum, harmonic engine noise, and non-stationary crowd babble\nalike, despite their structurally different inductive biases [12, 13]. (iii) Loosely controlled sampling\ncost. Generative SE pipelines often require many iterative refinement steps, with limited formal\nconnection between the training objective and the inference budget [10, 11].\nThe natural response, i.e., running a spectral and a waveform pathway in parallel and combining\nthem, fails on its own, because a generic ensemble of two prediction streams provides no mechanism\nto know which pathway is failing on a given input. Without such signal, the combination defaults\nto fixed-weight averaging, which is provably suboptimal whenever the two branches’ errors are\nuncorrelated, and the dual-domain promise reduces to a parameter-count increase.\nWe introduce HybridSB-MoE, a dual-domain framework organized around one asymmetric idea: a multiexpert spectral path naturally produces an epistemic uncertainty signal (which expert is right?), and a stochastic\nwaveform bridge naturally produces an aleatoric one\n(intrinsic transport noise). These are categorically different signals about categorically different errors. Pairing\nthe two pathways and fusing on this asymmetry yields\na mixing weight that selects between two error regimes\nrather than averaging two predictions, transforming the\ndual-domain promise into a principled co-design. Two\nfurther architectural choices are required for this idea to\nwork. First, the spectral pathway must produce informative expert disagreement, which requires architectural Figure 1: The persistent dual-domain diheterogeneity rather than capacity-replicated variation. chotomy in speech enhancement. WaveWe therefore use heterogeneous expert archetypes, each form processing preserves temporal fine\naligned with a canonical noise-processing primitive, in- structure and phase coherence but undercluding low-rank denoising, wide receptive fields, in- performs on harmonically structured information bottlenecks, harmonic bases, and universal terference; spectral processing captures\napproximation, and combine them under sparse routing. harmonic structure and stationary noise\nSecond, the waveform pathway must remain faithful un- patterns but fragments phase across STFT\nder the small inference budget K targeted by the system. frames. The two domains succeed and fail\nWe therefore train it with path-consistency and trajectory in opposite regimes, and existing generaregularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that jointly min- tive methods inherit one half of the tradeimizing them bounds the K-step bridge discretization off by committing to a single domain.\nerror in 2-Wasserstein distance, making small-K inference a consequence of the training objective\nrather than an empirical heuristic. In Section 3, we further show that no proper subset of these three\ncomponents recovers the central design property.\nOur contributions are as follows:\n1. A dual-domain framework built around asymmetric uncertainty. We propose HybridSB-MoE,\na dual-domain framework that couples spectral MoE and waveform SB pathways. By pairing each\npath with its natural uncertainty signal, epistemic disagreement for spectral experts and aleatoric\nvariance for the waveform bridge, HybridSB-MoE enables fusion that adapts to error regimes rather\nthan fixed-weight averaging.\n2. A heterogeneous spectral MoE for informative epistemic disagreement. We design a sparse\ntop-k=2 spectral MoE with architecturally distinct expert archetypes. This heterogeneity makes\nexpert disagreement reflect mismatched inductive biases rather than small perturbations among\ncapacity-replicated experts.\n3. A regularized waveform bridge for few-step inference. We train the waveform SB with\npath consistency and trajectory regularizers, and prove in Theorem 1 that they bound the K-step\ndiscretization error in 2-Wasserstein distance, linking small-K inference to the training objective.\n4. SOTA on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE improves over diffusion- and SB-based\nbaselines at their respective step budgets, remains competitive with consistency-distilled few-step\nmethods, and provides calibrated fusion uncertainty for input-adaptive dual-domain enhancement.\n\n2\n\n\f2\n\nRelated Work\n\nDiscriminative SE. The field has progressed from DNN masking [3, 4] that surpassed classical\nWiener and MMSE estimators [14, 15], through time-domain encoder–decoders (Conv-TasNet [5, 16],\nDEMUCS [2]), dual-path sequence models [6], full-sub-band hybrids (FullSubNet+ [7]), spectrotemporal grids (TF-GridNet [8]), and recent Mamba-based linear-complexity backbones [17, 18].\nJoint enhancement objectives that combine SI-SDR with perceptual surrogates push fidelity further\n[19, 20], yet the underlying paradigm remains a one-shot deterministic regression. Two limitations\ntherefore persist regardless of architecture. (i) Inherent ambiguity: under severe non-stationary\nor harmonically structured noise, the noisy-to-clean mapping admits multiple plausible solutions,\nand a deterministic regressor must collapse this multimodal posterior to a single point estimate,\nwhich typically manifests as residual noise or over-suppressed harmonics [21]. (ii) No uncertainty:\ndiscriminative networks expose no signal at inference time to flag this ambiguity, so a downstream\nmodule cannot tell when the prediction should be trusted—making them unsuitable as one branch of\na confidence-routed dual-domain combiner.\nGenerative models and Schrödinger Bridges for SE. Likelihood-based [21] and score-based\n[9, 22, 23] diffusion models reverse a noise corruption process; SGMSE/SGMSE+ [10, 11] apply\nthis in the complex spectrogram domain, jointly modeling magnitude and phase, but require many\niterative steps from an uninformative Gaussian prior. Two recent paradigms exploit the structure of\nthe noisy observation directly. Flow-matching approaches [24, 25] regress velocity fields between\ncoupled distributions, shortening the sampling trajectory but typically without an explicit stochastic\nperturbation. Schrödinger Bridges [26, 27] learn the optimal-transport-like coupling between the\nnoisy observation and clean speech, retaining a small bridge variance for full-support marginals.\nSB for SE [28, 29, 30, 31, 32] confirm improved low-SNR structure preservation along this shorter\npath. Empirical step-count reductions have been achieved by consistency distillation [33, 34],\nadversarial few-step training [35], and SB-consistency trajectory models [36]. None of these works\nlinks the training objective to the inference budget, where small K is asserted but not derived.\nTheoretical convergence analyses for diffusion sampling exist in the broader literature [37], but their\nbounds are stated in terms of the score-matching error rather than tractable, training-time-minimized\nregularizers, and have not been adapted to the doubly-conditioned SB setting. Moreover, these\nmethods remain single-domain and lack calibrated uncertainty for fusion. We address both with\nasymmetric uncertainty fusion and a discretization bound (Theorem 1) that links small-K inference\nto explicit training-time quantities.\nMixture-of-Experts for SE. MoE enables conditional computation with sparse routing, scaling\ncapacity without proportional inference cost [38, 39, 40]. In SE, sparse MoE [12] first enabled\nscene-adaptive processing via noise-clustered experts; zero-shot personalization [41] extended this to\nspeakers via embedding-based routing; clean-cluster pre-training [13] enforced expert specialization\nthrough pre-training initialization; and dynamic capacity allocation [42] improved routing efficiency\nat inference. However, this line of work shares three limitations. (i) Homogeneity. Existing methods\nreplicate a single architecture across experts, assuming one computational primitive can handle tonal,\nambient, harmonic, and crowd noise. This assumption breaks down under realistic, structurally\ndiverse noise. (ii) Routing without uncertainty. Existing routing only partitions inputs into specialist\nregions, rather than exposing interpretable uncertainty; thus, the epistemic signal from multi-expert\ndisagreement remains unused. (iii) Separation from generative SE. MoE routing and generative bridge\nmodels remain disconnected in SE, leaving scene-adaptive computation and structured distributional\ntransport as separate lines of work. We address all three with heterogeneous archetype experts that\nencode distinct noise-specific inductive biases. Their disagreement provides the epistemic signal\nwhich, together with the SB’s aleatoric variance, drives asymmetric fusion. To our knowledge, this is\nthe first joint MoE–SB framework for SE.\nUncertainty quantification and dual-branch fusion. Calibrated uncertainty for deep regression is\nmost commonly obtained via deep ensembles [43] or learned heteroscedastic variance heads, with\nclassification calibration assessed via ECE [44]. Prior dual-branch SE methods that combine timeand frequency-domain estimates typically use either fixed-weight averaging or learned attention over\npredictions [8, 2]; none assigns categorically distinct uncertainty types to the two branches, and so the\nfusion cannot route on which kind of error each branch is exposed to. Our pathway-typed asymmetric\nfusion is, to our knowledge, the first to exploit this structure, and the calibration loss (Eq. (11)) ties\nthe abstract epistemic/aleatoric labels to measurable per-pathway reconstruction errors so the routing\nweight is learned, not asserted.\n3\n\n\fFigure 2: Overview of HybridSB-MoE. The spectral pathway (top) routes log-magnitude features\nz = log |S{y}| through N heterogeneous archetype experts via gating G(z) to produce xspec (t).\nThe waveform pathway (bottom) iteratively refines an SB state [xt , y] through a U-Net to produce\nxwave (t). An uncertainty-aware fusion combines both via uepi (top-k expert disagreement) and uale\n(bridge-variance head) to yield x̂(t).\n\n3\n\nMethod\n\nHybridSB-MoE has three coupled components: a heterogeneous spectral MoE, an SB waveform\npathway with path-consistency and trajectory regularizers (for which we prove a K-step discretization\nbound, Theorem 1), and an asymmetric uncertainty fusion that selects between epistemic and aleatoric\nerror regimes. Figure 2 illustrates the overall HybridSB-MoE framework. We develop each component\nin turn and close the section by showing that no proper subset recovers the central design property.\nOverview. Given a noisy input y(t), HybridSB-MoE processes it through two parallel pathways\ndesigned to capture complementary speech characteristics. The spectral pathway applies the heterogeneous MoE to the log-magnitude STFT for scene-adaptive enhancement. In parallel, the waveform\npathway runs an efficient SB on the time-domain signal to preserve phase. The two pathways exhibit\nasymmetric reliability across acoustic conditions: spectral processing excels in harmonically structured noise, whereas waveform processing is more robust in phase-sensitive scenarios. Consequently,\na fixed fusion strategy is inherently suboptimal. We therefore introduce an uncertainty-aware fusion\nmodule that integrates xspec (t) and xwave (t) by dynamically weighting each pathway based on\nconfidence, producing the final enhanced signal x̂(t). Both pathways run in parallel at inference, and\nlong utterances use overlap-add segmentation (Appendix B).\nSpectral feature extraction. We apply the STFT [45] to obtain the complex spectrogram S{y} ∈\nCF ×Tf (F frequency bins) and decompose it in polar form into magnitude |S{y}| and phase ∠S{y},\nprocessed by separate heads (below). The MoE consumes log-magnitude features z = log |S{y}| ∈\nRF ×Tf , whose logarithmic scaling compresses the dynamic range in line with human hearing.\nHeterogeneous mixture-of-experts. Unlike conventional homogeneous MoE designs [38, 39, 40] in\nwhich all experts share the same architecture and differ only in learned weights, we instantiate distinct\narchitectural archetypes, each tailored to a canonical noise-processing primitive, i.e., Home, Nature,\nOffice, Transport, and Public for VoiceBank+DEMAND (see Appendix A for full specification).\nUnder sparse top-k routing, pairwise mixtures and weighted blends of these archetypes cover the 14\nnoise categories in VoiceBank+DEMAND [46] combinatorially rather than by a dedicated expert\nper noise, and the design extends to new acoustic environments by adding archetypes per noise\nfamily encountered, without altering the rest of the framework (see Appendix A for details). All\nexperts share a backbone of normalization (group norm [47]) → variable internal modules →\nbottleneck projection, ensuring routing-interface compatibility while permitting structurally divergent\nintermediate computation. This design, a small semantically anchored basis composed of sparse\n4\n\n\frouting, replaces the parameter scaling of large homogeneous MoEs with interpretable routing and\nmeaningful expert disagreement, which we use for asymmetric fusion below.\nScene-adaptive sparse routing. Standard sparse routing [38, 39] assigns each input token to its\ntop-k experts independently, which is well-suited to language modelling but ill-suited to SE: the\ndominant noise type is a global property of an utterance, while local acoustic events (a phoneme onset,\na transient hit) require frame-level adaptivity. We therefore use a two-level gate: Archetype-level\nrouting Garch (z) pools z across time and decides the dominant archetype mixture for the whole\nutterance; token-level routing Gtoken (z) refines this assignment per frame. The two combine as\nG(z) = αGarch (z) + (1 − α)Gtoken (z) with α ∈ [0, 1]. An MLP maps G(z) to per-expert logits;\nthe top-k entries are softmax-renormalized to weights {Gi (z)}i∈Ik , and the routed output is\nX\nx̂spec =\nGi (z) Ei (z),\n(1)\ni∈Ik\n\nwhere Ik indexes the selected experts and Ei are expert networks. To prevent expert collapse [38, 39],\na standard load-balancing regularizer\nLaux = λI Var(pi ) + λL Var(ni )\n\n(2)\n\npenalizes uneven utilization, where pi and ni denote the average routing probability and token count\nof expert i, respectively.\nSpectral reconstruction. The routed features x̂spec pass through two parallel 1 × 1-conv heads.\nSince phase cannot be inferred from log-magnitude alone, the phase head also consumes the noisy\nphase as (sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y}), concatenated along the channel dimension:\nM̂ = Mmax sigmoid(hmag (x̂spec )), ∆ϕ = ϕmax tanh(hpha (x̂spec , sin ∠S{y}, cos ∠S{y})), (3)\nwhere Mmax , ϕmax bound the mask and phase correction to prevent over-suppression, excessive\namplification, and phase-wrap instability. The enhanced spectrum Ŝ = M̂ ⊙ |S{y}| · ej(∠S{y}+∆ϕ)\nis inverted to xspec (t) via iSTFT with overlap-add. STFT processing nevertheless fragments temporal\nfine structure through frame-wise phase decoupling, motivating the parallel waveform pathway below.\nSchrödinger Bridge formulation. The continuous-time SB problem seeks the stochastic process\nminimizing the KL divergence to a reference Brownian motion subject to fixed marginal distributions\nat both endpoints [26, 27, 48]. In our application the two endpoints are the noisy observation y\nand the clean target x, so the bridge directly couples the conditional distributions our system must\ntransport between. Where standard diffusion [9] traverses the full path from a data-independent\nGaussian prior, the bridge exploits the substantial mutual information between y and x to yield a\nshorter, more informative trajectory and, empirically, fewer sampling steps and better low-SNR\nstructure preservation [28, 29, 30]. Conceptually adjacent flow-matching methods [24, 25] also\ncouple paired distributions but typically without an explicit bridge perturbation; our SB construction\nretains a small noise term σt ϵ to keep the marginal p(xt | x, y) full-support, which is required for\nscore-based reverse sampling and for the trajectory regularizer to act as a self-consistency constraint.\nThe intermediate state at diffusion time t ∈ [0, T ] interpolates between y and x with a small Gaussian\nperturbation,\nq\nq\nxt =\n\nβ̄t x +\n\n1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ,\n\nϵ ∼ N (0, I),\n\n(4)\n\nwhere β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +s)/(1+s)·π/2) is a cumulative cosine p\nschedule [49]\ndecreasing monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0 (offset s=0.008), and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n(σmax =0.05) vanishes at both endpoints, so x0 = x and xT = y deterministically while σt ϵ supplies\nthe full-support marginal p(xt | x, y) required by the score-based reverse process.\nThe reverse process is parameterized as a learnable denoiser x̂θ (xt , y, t) that predicts the clean target\ndirectly from the bridge state and the observation. Rather than solving the reverse-time stochastic\ndifferential equation (SDE) associated with the bridge in closed form, we adopt a tractable bridgeconsistent update that iteratively re-injects the predicted clean estimate into the forward construction\nof Eq. (4) at the previous timestep:\nq\nq\nxt−1 = β̄t−1 x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t−1 y + σt−1 z,\nz ∼ N (0, I).\n(5)\n5\n\n\fThis data-prediction update is the bridge analogue of DDPM/DDIM ancestral sampling [9, 23]: deterministic when σt−1 =0, stochastic otherwise. Bridge-marginal consistency is enforced empirically\nthrough the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced below. The denoiser is a 1D\nU-Net with transformer bottleneck and FiLM timestep conditioning (Appendix B). The training\nobjective is the data-prediction loss:\n2\n\nLdata\nSB = Et,(x,y),ϵ x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x 2 ,\n\n(6)\n\naugmented by the path-consistency and trajectory regularizers introduced next.\nPath-consistency and trajectory regularizers. A fixed front-loaded schedule tk = T (k/K)γ with\nγ<1 concentrates the K-step budget in the early-trajectory regime where the SNR changes fastest and\nwhere errors propagate to all later steps [9]. Two regularizers then encourage a smooth, low-curvature\ndenoising path in the (y, x) subspace. The path-consistency loss enforces cross-timestep agreement\nof clean-signal predictions along the same trajectory:\nh\ni\n2\nLpath = Et,t′ ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥\n(7)\nwhere t, t′ are independently sampled along the same bridge trajectory. This penalizes inconsistent\nclean-signal predictions across timesteps, reducing reverse-process curvature (cf. consistency models\n[34], but here for path smoothing, not distillation). The trajectory loss anchors xt to the scheduleconsistent reconstruction:\n\"\n#\nq\nq\n\u0001 2\nLtraj = Et xt −\nβ̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y\n.\n(8)\nAt the optimum x̂θ = x, the residual reduces to σt ϵ, so Ltraj couples the prediction to the specific\nbridge state xt at each timestep, complementing the in-expectation regression of Eq. (6). At inference,\nwe use K=8 steps with the front-loaded non-uniform discretization tk = T (k/K)γ , γ=0.6 (see\nAppendix B for more details).\nDiscretization bound. The two regularizers above make small-K inference viable, and we now show\nwhy. Let p̂K be the law of the K-step rollout of Eq. (5) at t=0 and pbr\n0 the continuous-time bridge\nmarginal at t=0, both conditional on y.\nAssumption 1 (Regularity).\np (i) x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and Lt -Lipschitz in\nt. (ii) β̄t and σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) are C 1 on [0, T ], with β̄t monotone and bounded second\nderivative. (iii) E∥xt ∥2 and E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 are uniformly bounded in t.\nTheorem 1 (K-step bridge discretization bound). Under Assumption 1, with the schedule tk =\nT (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\nq\n\u0001\n−α\nW2 p̂K , pbr\n≤\nC\nK\n+\nC\nL⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n(9)\n1\n2\n0\nwith α = min(1, γ), L⋆ the training-termination values of Eq. (7)–(8), and C1 , C2 depending only\non Lx , Lt , σmax , and the curvature of β̄t .\nWith α = min(1, γ), front-loading (γ < 1) concentrates steps near t=0 where Φ′ is largest, which\nis what drives small-K behavior empirically.\nEach term corresponds to a separate lever: C1 K −α is\n√\ncontrolled by inference-time K, γ; C2 L⋆ is controlled by training-time minimization of the two\n⋆\nregularizers and is independent of K.\n√ Two consequences follow. First, once L is small, the bound\n−α\nsaturates: any K with C1 K ≲ C2 L⋆ already attains the asymptotic floor, which is what justifies\nour K=8 rather than the much larger K of standard diffusion sampling. Second, the regularizers\nare not auxiliary terms; without them, the second term has no training-time upper bound, and the\ninequality does not exist as a function of K. Therefore, removing either breaks the entire argument.\nThis is the formal sense in which path-consistency and trajectory regularization are load-bearing\nfor our inference schedule. Eq. (9) is closely related to recent convergence results for diffusion\nsampling [37] but adapts those analyses to the doubly-conditioned bridge process and, importantly,\nreplaces score-matching error with the explicit, training-time-minimized regularizer pair, which\nmakes the bound directly actionable as a design tool. The full proof includes a one-step fidelity\nlemma combined with a non-uniform Riemann estimate under a synchronous coupling, with details\ndeferred to Appendix C. We present Eq. (9) as a design-justifying inequality, not a tight predictor.\n6\n\n\fAsymmetric uncertainty fusion. A naive dual-branch ensembler treats the two pathways symmetrically; ours does not, by design. Epistemic uncertainty (‘which expert is right?’) is well-defined only\nwhen multiple experts coexist (the spectral MoE); the waveform pathway has no analog. Aleatoric\nuncertainty (intrinsic stochasticity of the bridge SDE in Eq. (4)) is well-defined only when the\nforward process is genuinely stochastic (the waveform pathway); the deterministic spectral mask in\nEq. (3) has no analog. Each pathway therefore carries exactly one uncertainty type: uepi flags an\narchitectural mismatch, uale flags a stochastic transport error. The fusion selects\nP between these two\nerror regimes; it does not average two predictions. Concretely, uepi = kT1 f i∈Ik ∥Ei (z)− Ē(z)∥22\nP\nwith Ē(z) = k1 i∈Ik Ei (z) (deep-ensembles sense [43], not Bayesian posterior); uale is from the\nU-Net’s per-sample log-variance head, exponentiated and time-averaged. After z-score normalization,\na 2-layer MLP gives w = σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale )) ∈ [0, 1], and\nx̂(t) = w · xspec (t) + (1 − w) · xwave (t).\n\n(10)\n\nHigh expert disagreement pushes w toward the waveform pathway and high SB variance toward the\nspectral pathway: each pathway is deferred away from when its native error is large. A calibration\nloss anchors the two scalars to the errors they should track,\nLcal = (uepi − ∥xspec − x∥22 )2 + (uale − ∥xwave − x∥22 )2 ,\n\n(11)\n\nwhere uepi , uale are pre-normalized; without it, the scalars have no incentive to scale with error, and\nthe fusion’s dependence on them would be arbitrary.\nTraining. All components are trained end-to-end. Each step runs both pathways in parallel: the\nspectral path produces xspec , uepi ; the waveform path samples t, ϵ, forms xt via Eq. (4), and outputs\nxwave , uale . The fusion yields x̂. The objective combines reconstruction, SB, load-balancing, and\ncalibration losses:\nL = Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal ,\n(12)\n2\ndata\nwith Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥2 and LSB = LSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj . Loss weights (gridsearched on a 10% VB split): λSB =1.0, λpath =0.1, λtraj =0.05, λaux =0.01, λcal =0.05; ceilings\nMmax =5.0, ϕmax =π/4. Full procedure in Appendix B.\nWhy the components are coupled, not stacked. The central design property—a fusion that routes\non epistemic vs. aleatoric error regimes at a sampling budget derived from training—requires all\nthree components and survives no proper subset. Three counterfactuals make this concrete. (C1)\nSymmetric uncertainty heads on both pathways (the standard dual-branch ensemble): the fusion sees\ntwo aleatoric scalars and no epistemic one, making it blind to spectral expert disagreement. Thus,\nthe mixing weight collapses into a generic confidence average, consistent with the 0.17 PESQ drop\nobserved for “w/o uncertainty fusion” in Figure 3a. (C2) Homogeneous MoE in place of distinct\narchetypes: top-k blends span one function class instead of distinct inductive biases. In this way,\nuepi no longer indicates which bias is mismatched but only weight perturbations among identicalarchitecture experts, resulting in 0.43 PESQ drop as shown in Figure 3a. (C3) Drop the regularizers:\nTheorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1604}}}
