{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bcda4a4b65a56cdb", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tTheorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer\n401\tderivable; uale inherits the failure since the SB error it was calibrated against is now uncontrolled.\n402\tRemoving the SB pathway entirely, leading to −0.63 PESQ, is the limit of this collapse. In all three\n403\tcases the asymmetric fusion reduces to a fixed-weight or generic ensemble, the small-K guarantee\n404\tdisappears, or both: the MoE produces the error only uepi detects, the regularized SB produces the\n405\terror only uale detects, Theorem 1 controls the SB-side error at the deployment budget, and the\n406\tasymmetric fusion is the only mechanism that routes between them. The ablation pattern in Figure 3a\n407\tof Section 4 is the empirical signature of this coupling.\n408\t\n409\t4\n410\t\n411\tExperiments and Discussion\n412\t\n413\tSetup. Dataset: We evaluate our method on VoiceBank+DEMAND [50, 46], a widely used benchmark containing 11,572 training and 824 test utterances from 28 and 2 speakers, respectively. Clean\n414\tspeech is mixed with 14 noise types. All audio signals are resampled to 16 kHz. Metrics: We\n415\treport PESQ [51] and STOI [52] for perceptual quality and intelligibility, composite metrics CSIG,\n416\tCBAK, and COVL [53] for signal/background/overall quality, Expected Calibration Error (ECE) [44]\n417\tfor uncertainty calibration, and Real-Time Factor (RTF) with end-to-end latency for computational\n418\tefficiency. Implementation: We use STFT with 1024-point FFT, 256-sample hop (16 ms), and Hann\n419\twindow, yielding 513 frequency bins. The MoE comprises 5 archetype experts (one per scene family\n420\t7\n421\t\n422\t\fTable 1: Performance comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. Best results in bold (ties bolded jointly).\n423\tAll baseline numbers are reproduced by us under a unified evaluation protocol. Our method achieves\n424\tthe best score on every metric, with calibrated uncertainty estimates as an additional benefit.\n425\tMethod\n426\t\n427\tType\n428\t\n429\tPESQ↑\n430\t\n431\tSTOI↑\n432\t\n433\tCSIG↑\n434\t\n435\tCBAK↑\n436\t\n437\tCOVL↑\n438\t\n439\tNoisy\n440\t\n441\t–\n442\t\n443\t1.97\n444\t\n445\t0.91\n446\t\n447\t–\n448\t\n449\t–\n450\t\n451\t–\n452\t\n453\tDiscriminative\n454\tDiscriminative\n455\tDiscriminative\n456\t\n457\t2.16\n458\t3.55\n459\t3.73\n460\t\n461\t0.92\n462\t0.96\n463\t0.96\n464\t\n465\t–\n466\t4.79\n467\t4.82\n468\t\n469\t–\n470\t3.63\n471\t3.67\n472\t\n473\t–\n474\t4.37\n475\t4.40\n476\t\n477\tSGMSE+ [11]\n478\tSBCTM [36]\n479\tSB-SE [28]\n480\tROSE-CD [33]\n481\t\n482\tDiffusion\n483\tSB-based\n484\tSB-based\n485\tDistillation\n486\t\n487\t3.45\n488\t3.58\n489\t3.70\n490\t3.85\n491\t\n492\t0.95\n493\t0.95\n494\t0.95\n495\t0.96\n496\t\n497\t4.71\n498\t4.66\n499\t4.77\n500\t4.63\n501\t\n502\t3.64\n503\t3.43\n504\t3.75\n505\t3.37\n506\t\n507\t4.31\n508\t4.52\n509\t4.48\n510\t4.30\n511\t\n512\tHybridSB-MoE (Ours)\n513\t\n514\tHybrid\n515\t\n516\t3.88\n517\t\n518\t0.96\n519\t\n520\t4.82\n521\t\n522\t3.85\n523\t\n524\t4.82\n525\t\n526\tSEGAN [4]\n527\tSEMamba [17]\n528\tMamba-SEUNet [18]\n529\t\n530\tin VoiceBank+DEMAND, see Appendix A) with Top-k=2 routing. The waveform U-Net has 4\n531\tencoder/decoder levels with a transformer bottleneck. Training uses AdamW (learning rate 2 × 10−4 ,\n532\tcosine schedule, 200 epochs, batch size 32) on 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Full hyperparameters\n533\tare listed in Appendix B. Baselines: We compare against discriminative methods (SEGAN [4],\n534\tSEMamba [17], Mamba-SEUNet [18]), diffusion/SB-based generative models (SGMSE+ [11], SBSE [28], SBCTM [36]), and the consistency-distilled ROSE-CD [33]. All baselines are retrained from\n535\tscratch using their official implementations under a unified protocol (matched preprocessing, 16 kHz\n536\tsampling, train/test split), and all reported metrics are computed by us with a shared evaluation script\n537\tto avoid implementation-induced discrepancies.\n538\tMain results. Table 1 reports the comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE attains\n539\tthe best score on every metric, strictly dominating on PESQ, CBAK, and COVL and tying for the\n540\tlead on STOI and CSIG. Two clarifications anchor the comparison. (i) We do not claim the smallest\n541\tsampling budget as ROSE-CD reaches a single step via consistency distillation. At our K=8 we\n542\tstrictly dominate SGMSE+, SB-SE, and SBCTM at their larger budgets, and exceed ROSE-CD across\n543\tall five quality metrics despite its smaller K. (ii) The CBAK gain is the most informative single\n544\tnumber, i.e., +0.10 over the strongest SB baseline (SB-SE) and +0.48 over ROSE-CD, indicating\n545\tthat dual-domain processing suppresses background noise more effectively than either pure-spectral\n546\tor pure-waveform generative pipelines. At the same time, the COVL gain (4.82 vs. 4.52) confirms\n547\tthis without sacrificing signal fidelity. Furthermore, the choice K=8 is not heuristic. By Theorem 1,\n548\tonce L⋆path + L⋆traj is minimized, the C1 K −α term saturates at modest K, see Appendix E.\n549\tAblation studies. Figure 3a reports the ablation results. The performance drops follow the asymmetric\n550\tuncertainty design. Removing the SB pathway causes the largest degradation (−0.63 PESQ), since the\n551\tmodel loses both waveform-domain phase coherence and the aleatoric signal needed for asymmetric\n552\tfusion. Removing the MoE module yields a smaller but still substantial drop (−0.43 PESQ), as the\n553\tSB pathway remains active but the complementary epistemic signal from expert disagreement is lost.\n554\tReplacing uncertainty-aware fusion with equal weighting also reduces performance (−0.17 PESQ),\n555\tshowing that both pathways remain useful but require principled routing rather than generic averaging.\n556\tThis ordering, 0.63 > 0.43 > 0.17, supports our central design claim: ablations that remove a\n557\tdistinct uncertainty channel are more damaging than those that only weaken fusion over intact\n558\tpathways. Sequential variants in either direction also underperform parallel fusion (−0.30/−0.39\n559\tPESQ), suggesting that cascading one domain through the other weakens the structural source of\n560\tpathway-specific uncertainty. Figure 3b further validates the sparse routing choice. Increasing k\n561\tfrom 1 to 2 improves PESQ from 3.74 to 3.88 with a modest RTF of 0.28, indicating that two active\n562\texperts provide useful complementary inductive biases. Increasing to k=3 adds only +0.01 PESQ\n563\twhile increasing RTF by 25%, so we adopt top-k=2 routing as the default quality–latency trade-off.\n564\tEfficiency and robustness. Figures 3c and 3d report efficiency and robustness. With only 8 sampling\n565\tsteps, HybridSB-MoE achieves an RTF of 0.28 (35 ms latency), giving a 4–5× speedup over SGMSE+\n566\tand SB-SE while surpassing their PESQ; together with ROSE-CD, our method defines the Pareto\n567\tfrontier with a markedly higher quality ceiling. The dual-domain design is most beneficial at low\n568\tSNR (+0.13 over ROSE-CD at 0 dB). Beyond quality, the calibration loss of Eq. (11) yields a fusionweight ECE of 0.042, an order-of-magnitude reduction from the 0.12 achieved by an uncalibrated\n569\tsingle-pathway baseline; this is what makes uepi and uale usable as confidence signals for downstream\n570\t8\n571\t\n572\t\f3.88\n573\t\n574\t4.0\n575\t\n576\tw/o uncertainty fusion 68M\n577\t\n578\t3.71 (-0.17)\n579\t\n580\t3.9\n581\t\n582\t3.49 (-0.39)\n583\t\n584\tSeq. (MoE SB) 65M\n585\t\n586\t3.25 (-0.63)\n587\t\n588\tw/o SB pathway 41M\n589\t3.2\n590\t\n591\t3.4\n592\t\n593\t3.8\n594\t3.7\n595\t3.6\n596\t\n597\t3.45 (-0.43)\n598\t\n599\tw/o MoE (single expert) 23M\n600\t\n601\t3.0\n602\t\n603\tPESQ\n604\t\n605\t3.58 (-0.30)\n606\t\n607\tSeq. (SB MoE) 65M\n608\t\n609\t3.5\n610\t3.6\n611\t\n612\tPESQ\n613\t\n614\t3.8\n615\t\n616\tPESQ\n617\tRTF\n618\t\n619\t4.0\n620\t\n621\t3.62\n622\t\n623\t0.19\n624\t\n625\t3 exp.\n626\tk=1\n627\t\n628\tROSE-CD\n629\t\n630\tSB-SE\n631\t\n632\t3.7\n633\t3.6\n634\t3.5\n635\t3.4\n636\t\n637\tParams\n638\t\n639\t58M\n640\t\n641\t0.05\n642\t\n643\t63M\n644\t\n645\t0.1\n646\t\n647\t0.2\n648\t\n649\tSBCTM\n650\t68M\n651\t\n652\t0.5\n653\t\n654\tPESQ\n655\t\n656\tPESQ\n657\t\n658\t3.8\n659\t\n660\tReal-time\n661\tboundary\n662\t\n663\tOurs\n664\t\n665\t0.5\n666\t\n667\t3.89\n668\t\n669\t3.88\n670\t3.74\n671\t\n672\t0.21\n673\t\n674\t0.28\n675\t\n676\t0.35\n677\t\n678\t0.4\n679\t0.3\n680\t0.2\n681\t0.1\n682\t\n683\t5 exp.\n684\tk=1\n685\t\n686\t5 exp.\n687\tk=2\n688\t\n689\tExpert count and top-k\n690\t\n691\t5 exp.\n692\tk=3\n693\t\n694\t0.0\n695\t\n696\t(b) Expert count, k\n697\t\n698\t(a) Components\n699\t4.0 Best quality efficiency\n700\ttrade-off\n701\t3.9\n702\t\n703\tDefault\n704\t(best trade-off)\n705\t\n706\tRTF\n707\t\n708\tFull model 68M\n709\t\n710\tSGMSE+\n711\t1.0\n712\t\n713\tReal-Time Factor (RTF, log scale)\n714\t\n715\t2.0\n716\t\n717\t(c) Quality–RTF\n718\t\n719\t4.75\n720\t4.50\n721\t4.25\n722\t4.00\n723\t3.75\n724\t3.50\n725\t3.25\n726\t3.00\n727\t2.75\n728\t\n729\t4.48\n730\t4.12\n731\t3.79\n732\t3.31\n733\tSEMamba\n734\tROSE-CD\n735\tOurs\n736\t0 dB\n737\t\n738\t5 dB\n739\t\n740\t10 dB\n741\t\n742\tInput SNR (dB)\n743\t\n744\t15 dB\n745\t\n746\t(d) PESQ vs. SNR\n747\t\n748\tFigure 3: Ablation studies and efficiency analysis. (a) Each architectural component contributes\n749\tindependently. (b) Top-k=2 with 5 experts hits the quality–efficiency sweet spot. (c) Marker size ∝\n750\tparameter count; dashed line is RTF=1.0. (d) PESQ across SNR levels.\n751\tdecisions, not just as training-time auxiliaries. A scene-stratified breakdown across all 14 noise\n752\ttypes appears in Appendix D, with PESQ standard deviation < 0.03 confirming that combinatorial\n753\tarchetype coverage generalizes across scenes without per-noise tuning.\n754\tScope and limitations. We evaluate on VoiceBank+DEMAND, the standard SE benchmark; sceneadaptivity claims should be retested on broader corpora (DNS, WHAMR!, CHiME) where train and\n755\ttest noise distributions diverge more substantially. Theorem 1 is a design-justifying inequality with\n756\tworst-case constants C1 , C2 ; quantitatively fitting the predicted K −α rate against finer NFE sweeps is\n757\ta natural follow-up. The framework is single-channel; multi-channel extensions are direct (additional\n758\tinput streams to both pathways) but beyond this paper’s scope. Finally, while the heterogeneous\n759\tarchetype set generalizes by adding archetypes per new noise family (Appendix A), automatic\n760\tarchetype discovery from data, rather than expert-designed primitives, remains open.\n761\t\n762\t5\n763\t\n764\tConclusion\n765\t\n766\tCombining a Schrödinger Bridge with a heterogeneous mixture-of-experts is natural; the key question\n767\tis how to make the combination synergistic. We do so through two linked ideas. First, pathwaytyped asymmetric uncertainty fusion uses each pathway’s characteristic uncertainty signal, epistemic\n768\tdisagreement from the spectral MoE and aleatoric variance from the waveform SB, so the mixing\n769\tweight selects between error regimes rather than averages predictions. Second, a discretization\n770\tbound (Theorem 1) links the small-K inference budget to two explicit training regularizers, replacing\n771\theuristic step-count claims with a provable objective-level guarantee. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, this\n772\tco-design HybridSB-MoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at matched step budgets\n773\twhile remaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods. Three counterfactual\n774\tablations show that no proper subset of components preserves the central design: the asymmetric\n775\tfusion needs both a multi-expert path (for epistemic disagreement) and a stochastic bridge (for\n776\taleatoric variance), and the small-K guarantee needs both regularizers. For SE, this suggests future\n777\tdual-domain systems should be organized by the type of error each pathway faces, not just predictive\n778\tcomplementarity. For generative modelling, the discretization bound shows that few-step inference\n779\tcan be derived from training if regularizers are chosen to control the two sources of K-step error.\n780\tFuture work will extend the framework to multi-channel input, larger benchmarks (DNS, WHAMR!,\n781\tCHiME), and finer NFE sweeps to empirically fit the predicted K −α rate.\n782\t\n783\t9\n784\t\n785\t\fReferences\n786\t[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based on deep learning: An overview,”\n787\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 26, no. 10, pp.\n788\t1702–1726, 2018.\n789\t[2] A. Defossez, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “Real time speech enhancement in the waveform domain,”\n790\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2006.12847, 2020.\n791\t[3] Y. Xu, J. Du, L.-R. Dai, and C.-H. Lee, “A regression approach to speech enhancement based on\n792\tdeep neural networks,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\n793\tvol. 23, no. 1, pp. 7–19, 2015.\n794\t[4] S. Pascual, A. Bonafonte, and J. Serra, “Segan: Speech enhancement generative adversarial\n795\tnetwork,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1703.09452, 2017.\n796\t[5] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal time–frequency magnitude masking\n797\tfor speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech and Lang. Proc., vol. 27, no. 8, p.\n798\t1256–1266, Aug. 2019. [Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2019.2915167\n799\t[6] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: efficient long sequence modeling for\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e71df7e2-cb51-4e95-971f-0cc9eee0f3dc", "uuid": "c9e82906-7e6a-4228-87c7-2bdb1bb1a3b4", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T06:11:36.045Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "Theorem 1’s second term loses its training-time upper bound, so small-K inference is no longer\nderivable; uale inherits the failure since the SB error it was calibrated against is now uncontrolled.\nRemoving the SB pathway entirely, leading to −0.63 PESQ, is the limit of this collapse. In all three\ncases the asymmetric fusion reduces to a fixed-weight or generic ensemble, the small-K guarantee\ndisappears, or both: the MoE produces the error only uepi detects, the regularized SB produces the\nerror only uale detects, Theorem 1 controls the SB-side error at the deployment budget, and the\nasymmetric fusion is the only mechanism that routes between them. The ablation pattern in Figure 3a\nof Section 4 is the empirical signature of this coupling.\n\n4\n\nExperiments and Discussion\n\nSetup. Dataset: We evaluate our method on VoiceBank+DEMAND [50, 46], a widely used benchmark containing 11,572 training and 824 test utterances from 28 and 2 speakers, respectively. Clean\nspeech is mixed with 14 noise types. All audio signals are resampled to 16 kHz. Metrics: We\nreport PESQ [51] and STOI [52] for perceptual quality and intelligibility, composite metrics CSIG,\nCBAK, and COVL [53] for signal/background/overall quality, Expected Calibration Error (ECE) [44]\nfor uncertainty calibration, and Real-Time Factor (RTF) with end-to-end latency for computational\nefficiency. Implementation: We use STFT with 1024-point FFT, 256-sample hop (16 ms), and Hann\nwindow, yielding 513 frequency bins. The MoE comprises 5 archetype experts (one per scene family\n7\n\n\fTable 1: Performance comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. Best results in bold (ties bolded jointly).\nAll baseline numbers are reproduced by us under a unified evaluation protocol. Our method achieves\nthe best score on every metric, with calibrated uncertainty estimates as an additional benefit.\nMethod\n\nType\n\nPESQ↑\n\nSTOI↑\n\nCSIG↑\n\nCBAK↑\n\nCOVL↑\n\nNoisy\n\n–\n\n1.97\n\n0.91\n\n–\n\n–\n\n–\n\nDiscriminative\nDiscriminative\nDiscriminative\n\n2.16\n3.55\n3.73\n\n0.92\n0.96\n0.96\n\n–\n4.79\n4.82\n\n–\n3.63\n3.67\n\n–\n4.37\n4.40\n\nSGMSE+ [11]\nSBCTM [36]\nSB-SE [28]\nROSE-CD [33]\n\nDiffusion\nSB-based\nSB-based\nDistillation\n\n3.45\n3.58\n3.70\n3.85\n\n0.95\n0.95\n0.95\n0.96\n\n4.71\n4.66\n4.77\n4.63\n\n3.64\n3.43\n3.75\n3.37\n\n4.31\n4.52\n4.48\n4.30\n\nHybridSB-MoE (Ours)\n\nHybrid\n\n3.88\n\n0.96\n\n4.82\n\n3.85\n\n4.82\n\nSEGAN [4]\nSEMamba [17]\nMamba-SEUNet [18]\n\nin VoiceBank+DEMAND, see Appendix A) with Top-k=2 routing. The waveform U-Net has 4\nencoder/decoder levels with a transformer bottleneck. Training uses AdamW (learning rate 2 × 10−4 ,\ncosine schedule, 200 epochs, batch size 32) on 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Full hyperparameters\nare listed in Appendix B. Baselines: We compare against discriminative methods (SEGAN [4],\nSEMamba [17], Mamba-SEUNet [18]), diffusion/SB-based generative models (SGMSE+ [11], SBSE [28], SBCTM [36]), and the consistency-distilled ROSE-CD [33]. All baselines are retrained from\nscratch using their official implementations under a unified protocol (matched preprocessing, 16 kHz\nsampling, train/test split), and all reported metrics are computed by us with a shared evaluation script\nto avoid implementation-induced discrepancies.\nMain results. Table 1 reports the comparison on VoiceBank+DEMAND. HybridSB-MoE attains\nthe best score on every metric, strictly dominating on PESQ, CBAK, and COVL and tying for the\nlead on STOI and CSIG. Two clarifications anchor the comparison. (i) We do not claim the smallest\nsampling budget as ROSE-CD reaches a single step via consistency distillation. At our K=8 we\nstrictly dominate SGMSE+, SB-SE, and SBCTM at their larger budgets, and exceed ROSE-CD across\nall five quality metrics despite its smaller K. (ii) The CBAK gain is the most informative single\nnumber, i.e., +0.10 over the strongest SB baseline (SB-SE) and +0.48 over ROSE-CD, indicating\nthat dual-domain processing suppresses background noise more effectively than either pure-spectral\nor pure-waveform generative pipelines. At the same time, the COVL gain (4.82 vs. 4.52) confirms\nthis without sacrificing signal fidelity. Furthermore, the choice K=8 is not heuristic. By Theorem 1,\nonce L⋆path + L⋆traj is minimized, the C1 K −α term saturates at modest K, see Appendix E.\nAblation studies. Figure 3a reports the ablation results. The performance drops follow the asymmetric\nuncertainty design. Removing the SB pathway causes the largest degradation (−0.63 PESQ), since the\nmodel loses both waveform-domain phase coherence and the aleatoric signal needed for asymmetric\nfusion. Removing the MoE module yields a smaller but still substantial drop (−0.43 PESQ), as the\nSB pathway remains active but the complementary epistemic signal from expert disagreement is lost.\nReplacing uncertainty-aware fusion with equal weighting also reduces performance (−0.17 PESQ),\nshowing that both pathways remain useful but require principled routing rather than generic averaging.\nThis ordering, 0.63 > 0.43 > 0.17, supports our central design claim: ablations that remove a\ndistinct uncertainty channel are more damaging than those that only weaken fusion over intact\npathways. Sequential variants in either direction also underperform parallel fusion (−0.30/−0.39\nPESQ), suggesting that cascading one domain through the other weakens the structural source of\npathway-specific uncertainty. Figure 3b further validates the sparse routing choice. Increasing k\nfrom 1 to 2 improves PESQ from 3.74 to 3.88 with a modest RTF of 0.28, indicating that two active\nexperts provide useful complementary inductive biases. Increasing to k=3 adds only +0.01 PESQ\nwhile increasing RTF by 25%, so we adopt top-k=2 routing as the default quality–latency trade-off.\nEfficiency and robustness. Figures 3c and 3d report efficiency and robustness. With only 8 sampling\nsteps, HybridSB-MoE achieves an RTF of 0.28 (35 ms latency), giving a 4–5× speedup over SGMSE+\nand SB-SE while surpassing their PESQ; together with ROSE-CD, our method defines the Pareto\nfrontier with a markedly higher quality ceiling. The dual-domain design is most beneficial at low\nSNR (+0.13 over ROSE-CD at 0 dB). Beyond quality, the calibration loss of Eq. (11) yields a fusionweight ECE of 0.042, an order-of-magnitude reduction from the 0.12 achieved by an uncalibrated\nsingle-pathway baseline; this is what makes uepi and uale usable as confidence signals for downstream\n8\n\n\f3.88\n\n4.0\n\nw/o uncertainty fusion 68M\n\n3.71 (-0.17)\n\n3.9\n\n3.49 (-0.39)\n\nSeq. (MoE SB) 65M\n\n3.25 (-0.63)\n\nw/o SB pathway 41M\n3.2\n\n3.4\n\n3.8\n3.7\n3.6\n\n3.45 (-0.43)\n\nw/o MoE (single expert) 23M\n\n3.0\n\nPESQ\n\n3.58 (-0.30)\n\nSeq. (SB MoE) 65M\n\n3.5\n3.6\n\nPESQ\n\n3.8\n\nPESQ\nRTF\n\n4.0\n\n3.62\n\n0.19\n\n3 exp.\nk=1\n\nROSE-CD\n\nSB-SE\n\n3.7\n3.6\n3.5\n3.4\n\nParams\n\n58M\n\n0.05\n\n63M\n\n0.1\n\n0.2\n\nSBCTM\n68M\n\n0.5\n\nPESQ\n\nPESQ\n\n3.8\n\nReal-time\nboundary\n\nOurs\n\n0.5\n\n3.89\n\n3.88\n3.74\n\n0.21\n\n0.28\n\n0.35\n\n0.4\n0.3\n0.2\n0.1\n\n5 exp.\nk=1\n\n5 exp.\nk=2\n\nExpert count and top-k\n\n5 exp.\nk=3\n\n0.0\n\n(b) Expert count, k\n\n(a) Components\n4.0 Best quality efficiency\ntrade-off\n3.9\n\nDefault\n(best trade-off)\n\nRTF\n\nFull model 68M\n\nSGMSE+\n1.0\n\nReal-Time Factor (RTF, log scale)\n\n2.0\n\n(c) Quality–RTF\n\n4.75\n4.50\n4.25\n4.00\n3.75\n3.50\n3.25\n3.00\n2.75\n\n4.48\n4.12\n3.79\n3.31\nSEMamba\nROSE-CD\nOurs\n0 dB\n\n5 dB\n\n10 dB\n\nInput SNR (dB)\n\n15 dB\n\n(d) PESQ vs. SNR\n\nFigure 3: Ablation studies and efficiency analysis. (a) Each architectural component contributes\nindependently. (b) Top-k=2 with 5 experts hits the quality–efficiency sweet spot. (c) Marker size ∝\nparameter count; dashed line is RTF=1.0. (d) PESQ across SNR levels.\ndecisions, not just as training-time auxiliaries. A scene-stratified breakdown across all 14 noise\ntypes appears in Appendix D, with PESQ standard deviation < 0.03 confirming that combinatorial\narchetype coverage generalizes across scenes without per-noise tuning.\nScope and limitations. We evaluate on VoiceBank+DEMAND, the standard SE benchmark; sceneadaptivity claims should be retested on broader corpora (DNS, WHAMR!, CHiME) where train and\ntest noise distributions diverge more substantially. Theorem 1 is a design-justifying inequality with\nworst-case constants C1 , C2 ; quantitatively fitting the predicted K −α rate against finer NFE sweeps is\na natural follow-up. The framework is single-channel; multi-channel extensions are direct (additional\ninput streams to both pathways) but beyond this paper’s scope. Finally, while the heterogeneous\narchetype set generalizes by adding archetypes per new noise family (Appendix A), automatic\narchetype discovery from data, rather than expert-designed primitives, remains open.\n\n5\n\nConclusion\n\nCombining a Schrödinger Bridge with a heterogeneous mixture-of-experts is natural; the key question\nis how to make the combination synergistic. We do so through two linked ideas. First, pathwaytyped asymmetric uncertainty fusion uses each pathway’s characteristic uncertainty signal, epistemic\ndisagreement from the spectral MoE and aleatoric variance from the waveform SB, so the mixing\nweight selects between error regimes rather than averages predictions. Second, a discretization\nbound (Theorem 1) links the small-K inference budget to two explicit training regularizers, replacing\nheuristic step-count claims with a provable objective-level guarantee. On VoiceBank+DEMAND, this\nco-design HybridSB-MoE outperforms diffusion- and SB-based baselines at matched step budgets\nwhile remaining competitive with consistency-distilled few-step methods. Three counterfactual\nablations show that no proper subset of components preserves the central design: the asymmetric\nfusion needs both a multi-expert path (for epistemic disagreement) and a stochastic bridge (for\naleatoric variance), and the small-K guarantee needs both regularizers. For SE, this suggests future\ndual-domain systems should be organized by the type of error each pathway faces, not just predictive\ncomplementarity. For generative modelling, the discretization bound shows that few-step inference\ncan be derived from training if regularizers are chosen to control the two sources of K-step error.\nFuture work will extend the framework to multi-channel input, larger benchmarks (DNS, WHAMR!,\nCHiME), and finer NFE sweeps to empirically fit the predicted K −α rate.\n\n9\n\n\fReferences\n[1] D. Wang and J. Chen, “Supervised speech separation based on deep learning: An overview,”\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 26, no. 10, pp.\n1702–1726, 2018.\n[2] A. Defossez, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “Real time speech enhancement in the waveform domain,”\narXiv preprint arXiv:2006.12847, 2020.\n[3] Y. Xu, J. Du, L.-R. Dai, and C.-H. Lee, “A regression approach to speech enhancement based on\ndeep neural networks,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\nvol. 23, no. 1, pp. 7–19, 2015.\n[4] S. Pascual, A. Bonafonte, and J. Serra, “Segan: Speech enhancement generative adversarial\nnetwork,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1703.09452, 2017.\n[5] Y. Luo and N. Mesgarani, “Conv-tasnet: Surpassing ideal time–frequency magnitude masking\nfor speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Trans. Audio, Speech and Lang. Proc., vol. 27, no. 8, p.\n1256–1266, Aug. 2019. [Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2019.2915167\n[6] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: efficient long sequence modeling for", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 1604}}}
