{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9e6023c09cb5980d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "800\ttime-domain single-channel speech separation,” in ICASSP, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n801\t[7] J. Chen, Z. Wang, D. Tuo, Z. Wu, S. Kang, and H. Meng, “Fullsubnet+: Channel attention\n802\tfullsubnet with complex spectrograms for speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2022-2022 IEEE\n803\tInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022,\n804\tpp. 7857–7861.\n805\t[8] Z.-Q. Wang, S. Cornell, S. Choi, Y. Lee, B.-Y. Kim, and S. Watanabe, “Tf-gridnet: Integrating\n806\tfull- and sub-band modeling for speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech,\n807\tand Language Processing, vol. 31, pp. 3221–3236, 2023.\n808\t[9] J. Ho, A. Jain, and P. Abbeel, “Denoising diffusion probabilistic models,” in Proceedings of the\n809\t34th International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, ser. NIPS ’20. Red\n810\tHook, NY, USA: Curran Associates Inc., 2020.\n811\t[10] S. Welker, J. Richter, and T. Gerkmann, “Speech enhancement with score-based generative\n812\tmodels in the complex stft domain,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2203.17004, 2022.\n813\t[11] J. Richter, S. Welker, J.-M. Lemercier, B. Lay, and T. Gerkmann, “Speech enhancement and\n814\tdereverberation with diffusion-based generative models,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\n815\tSpeech, and Language Processing, vol. 31, pp. 2351–2364, 2023.\n816\t[12] A. Sivaraman and M. Kim, “Sparse mixture of local experts for efficient speech enhancement,”\n817\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2005.08128, 2020.\n818\t[13] S. E. Chazan, J. Goldberger, and S. Gannot, “Speech enhancement with mixture of deep experts\n819\twith clean clustering pre-training,” in ICASSP 2021 - 2021 IEEE International Conference on\n820\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2021, pp. 716–720.\n821\t[14] S. Boll, “Suppression of acoustic noise in speech using spectral subtraction,” IEEE Transactions\n822\ton Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing, vol. 27, no. 2, pp. 113–120, 1979.\n823\t[15] Y. Ephraim and D. Malah, “Speech enhancement using a minimum-mean square error short-time\n824\tspectral amplitude estimator,” IEEE Transactions on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing,\n825\tvol. 32, no. 6, pp. 1109–1121, 1984.\n826\t[16] J. L. Roux, S. Wisdom, H. Erdogan, and J. R. Hershey, “Sdr – half-baked or well done?” in\n827\tICASSP 2019 - 2019 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n828\t(ICASSP), 2019, pp. 626–630.\n829\t[17] R. Chao, W.-H. Cheng, M. La Quatra, S. M. Siniscalchi, C.-H. H. Yang, S.-W. Fu, and Y. Tsao,\n830\t“An investigation of incorporating mamba for speech enhancement,” in 2024 IEEE Spoken\n831\tLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT). IEEE, 2024, pp. 302–308.\n832\t10\n833\t\n834\t\f[18] J. Wang, Z. Lin, T. Wang, M. Ge, L. Wang, and J. Dang, “Mamba-seunet: Mamba unet for\n835\tmonaural speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on\n836\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025, pp. 1–5.\n837\t[19] H. Shi, K. Shimada, M. Hirano, T. Shibuya, Y. Koyama, Z. Zhong, S. Takahashi, T. Kawahara,\n838\tand Y. Mitsufuji, “Diffusion-based speech enhancement with joint generative and predictive\n839\tdecoders,” in ICASSP 2024 - 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and\n840\tSignal Processing (ICASSP), 2024, pp. 12 951–12 955.\n841\t[20] S. T. Yousif and B. M. Mahmmod, “Speech enhancement algorithms: A systematic literature\n842\treview,” Algorithms, vol. 18, no. 5, p. 272, 2025.\n843\t[21] T. Chen, G.-H. Liu, and E. A. Theodorou, “Likelihood training of schrödinger bridge using\n844\tforward–backward sdes theory,” in ICLR, 2022.\n845\t[22] Y. Song, J. Sohl-Dickstein, D. P. Kingma, A. Kumar, S. Ermon, and B. Poole, “Score-based\n846\tgenerative modeling through stochastic differential equations,” in International Conference\n847\ton Learning Representations, 2021. [Online]. Available: https://openreview.net/forum?id=\n848\tPxTIG12RRHS\n849\t[23] J. Song, C. Meng, and S. Ermon, “Denoising diffusion implicit models,” arXiv preprint\n850\tarXiv:2010.02502, 2020.\n851\t[24] Y. Lipman, R. T. Q. Chen, H. Ben-Hamu, M. Nickel, and M. Le, “Flow matching for generative\n852\tmodeling,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n853\t[25] X. Liu, C. Gong, and Q. Liu, “Flow straight and fast: Learning to generate and transfer data\n854\twith rectified flow,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n855\t[26] V. De Bortoli, J. Thornton, J. Heng, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion Schrödinger bridge with\n856\tapplications to score-based generative modeling,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing\n857\tSystems, vol. 34, 2021, pp. 17 695–17 709.\n858\t[27] Y. Shi, V. De Bortoli, A. Campbell, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion schrödinger bridge matching,”\n859\tin Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, A. Oh, T. Naumann, A. Globerson,\n860\tK. Saenko, M. Hardt, and S. Levine, Eds., vol. 36. Curran Associates, Inc., 2023, pp.\n861\t62 183–62 223. [Online]. Available: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/\n862\tc428adf74782c2092d254329b6b02482-Paper-Conference.pdf\n863\t[28] A. Jukić, R. Korostik, J. Balam, and B. Ginsburg, “Schrödinger bridge for generative speech\n864\tenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.16074, 2024.\n865\t[29] S. Wang, S. Liu, A. Harper, P. Kendrick, M. Salzmann, and M. Cernak, “Diffusion-based\n866\tspeech enhancement with Schrödinger bridge and symmetric noise schedule,” arXiv preprint\n867\tarXiv:2409.05116, 2024.\n868\t[30] Z. Tang, T. Hang, S. Gu, D. Chen, and B. Guo, “Simplified diffusion schrödinger bridge,” arXiv\n869\tpreprint arXiv:2403.14623, 2024.\n870\t[31] J. Yang, S. Wang, C. Wu, L. Guo, and F. Fan, “Schrödinger bridge mamba for one-step speech\n871\tenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.16834, 2025.\n872\t[32] H. Zhang, G. Li, P. Wu, Y. Gao, and H. Zhang, “Sb-senet: Diffusion model based on schrödinger\n873\tbridge for speech enhancement,” Applied Acoustics, vol. 236, p. 110742, 2025.\n874\t[33] L. Xu, L. F. Yan, and W. B. Kleijn, “Robust one-step speech enhancement via consistency\n875\tdistillation,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.05688, 2025.\n876\t[34] Y. Song, P. Dhariwal, M. Chen, and I. Sutskever, “Consistency models,” in International\n877\tConference on Machine Learning, 2023.\n878\t[35] S. Han, S. Lee, J. Lee, and K. Lee, “Few-step adversarial schrödinger bridge for generative\n879\tspeech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.01460, 2025.\n880\t11\n881\t\n882\t\f[36] S. Nishigori, K. Saito, N. Murata, M. Hirano, S. Takahashi, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n883\t“Schrödinger bridge consistency trajectory models for speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint\n884\tarXiv:2507.11925, 2025.\n885\t[37] S. Chen, S. Chewi, J. Li, Y. Li, A. Salim, and A. R. Zhang, “Sampling is as easy as learning the\n886\tscore: theory for diffusion models with minimal data assumptions,” in International Conference\n887\ton Learning Representations, 2023.\n888\t[38] N. Shazeer, A. Mirhoseini, K. Maziarz, A. Davis, Q. Le, G. Hinton, and J. Dean, “Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer,” arXiv preprint\n889\tarXiv:1701.06538, 2017.\n890\t[39] W. Fedus, B. Zoph, and N. Shazeer, “Switch transformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models\n891\twith simple and efficient sparsity,” Journal of Machine Learning Research, vol. 23, no. 120, pp.\n892\t1–39, 2022. [Online]. Available: http://jmlr.org/papers/v23/21-0998.html\n893\t[40] D. Lepikhin, H. Lee, Y. Xu, D. Chen, O. Firat, Y. Huang, M. Krikun, N. Shazeer, and Z. Chen,\n894\t“Gshard: Scaling giant models with conditional computation and automatic sharding,” arXiv\n895\tpreprint arXiv:2006.16668, 2020.\n896\t[41] A. Sivaraman and M. Kim, “Zero-shot personalized speech enhancement through speakerinformed model selection,” in 2021 IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to\n897\tAudio and Acoustics (WASPAA), 2021, pp. 171–175.\n898\t[42] R. Miccini, M. Kim, C. Laroche, L. Pezzarossa, and P. Smaragdis, “Adaptive slimming for\n899\tscalable and efficient speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.04879, 2025.\n900\t[43] B. Lakshminarayanan, A. Pritzel, and C. Blundell, “Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty\n901\testimation using deep ensembles,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems,\n902\tvol. 30, 2017.\n903\t[44] C. Guo, G. Pleiss, Y. Sun, and K. Q. Weinberger, “On calibration of modern neural networks,”\n904\tin Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Machine Learning - Volume 70, ser.\n905\tICML’17. JMLR.org, 2017, p. 1321–1330.\n906\t[45] J. B. Allen and L. R. Rabiner, “A unified approach to short-time fourier analysis and synthesis,”\n907\tProceedings of the IEEE, vol. 65, no. 11, pp. 1558–1564, 1977.\n908\t[46] J. Thiemann, N. Ito, and E. Vincent, “The diverse environments multi-channel acoustic\n909\tnoise database (demand): A database of multichannel environmental noise recordings,”\n910\tProceedings of Meetings on Acoustics, vol. 19, no. 1, p. 035081, 05 2013. [Online]. Available:\n911\thttps://doi.org/10.1121/1.4799597\n912\t[47] Y. Wu and K. He, “Group normalization,” in Proceedings of the European Conference on\n913\tComputer Vision, 2018, pp. 3–19.\n914\t[48] G. Peyre and M. Cuturi, “Computational optimal transport,” Foundations and Trends in Machine\n915\tLearning, vol. 11, no. 5-6, pp. 355–607, 2019.\n916\t[49] A. Nichol and P. Dhariwal, “Improved denoising diffusion probabilistic models,” 2021.\n917\t[Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09672\n918\t[50] C. Valentini-Botinhao, X. Wang, S. Takaki, and J. Yamagishi, “Investigating rnn-based speech\n919\tenhancement methods for noise-robust text-to-speech,” in 9th ISCA Workshop on Speech\n920\tSynthesis Workshop (SSW 9), 2016, pp. 146–152.\n921\t[51] A. Rix, J. Beerends, M. Hollier, and A. Hekstra, “Perceptual evaluation of speech quality\n922\t(pesq)-a new method for speech quality assessment of telephone networks and codecs,” in 2001\n923\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing. Proceedings (Cat.\n924\tNo.01CH37221), vol. 2, 2001, pp. 749–752 vol.2.\n925\t[52] C. H. Taal, R. C. Hendriks, R. Heusdens, and J. Jensen, “An algorithm for intelligibility\n926\tprediction of time–frequency weighted noisy speech,” IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech,\n927\tand Language Processing, vol. 19, no. 7, pp. 2125–2136, 2011.\n928\t12\n929\t\n930\t\f[53] Y. Hu and P. C. Loizou, “Evaluation of objective quality measures for speech enhancement,”\n931\tIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 229–238,\n932\t2008.\n933\t[54] N. Tishby and N. Zaslavsky, “Deep learning and the information bottleneck principle,” in 2015\n934\tIEEE Information Theory Workshop (ITW), 2015, pp. 1–5.\n935\t\n936\t13\n937\t\n938\t\fA\n939\t\n940\tHeterogeneous Expert Architectures\n941\t\n942\tThis appendix details the heterogeneous MoE layer described in Section 3 of the main paper. We\n943\tdescribe the design philosophy that connects each architectural archetype to a canonical noise family,\n944\tlist the configurations used for VoiceBank+DEMAND, and discuss how the archetype set generalizes\n945\tto other datasets.\n946\tA.1\n947\t\n948\tDesign Philosophy\n949\t\n950\tThe heterogeneous MoE is grounded in the empirical observation that real-world acoustic noise,\n951\tdespite its surface diversity, can be decomposed into a small number of canonical processing primitives. Stationary tonal noise (e.g., kitchen appliances, refrigerator hum) is well-modeled by compact\n952\tnormalized layers with low-rank structure; ambient natural backgrounds (e.g., park, river, field)\n953\tbenefit from wide receptive fields with strong normalization to capture diffuse spectral content; office\n954\tand meeting environments contain overlapping voices and structured interference best handled by\n955\tinformation bottlenecks [54]; transportation noise (bus, car, metro) exhibits strong harmonic and\n956\tquasi-periodic energy from engines and motors, naturally captured by harmonic basis expansion; and\n957\tunstructured public-space mixtures (cafeteria, station, restaurant) require universal-approximationstyle general-purpose blocks. Rather than scaling capacity by replicating one architecture (as in\n958\thomogeneous MoE), we instantiate one expert per archetype, then rely on sparse routing to combine\n959\tthem per input.\n960\tFigure 4 (reproduced here for completeness) illustrates the shared backbone and the architecturally\n961\tdistinct instantiations. All experts pass features through LayerNorm → variable internal modules\n962\t→ bottleneck projection, ensuring interface compatibility for routing while permitting structurally\n963\tdivergent computation in the middle.\n964\t\n965\tFigure 4: Heterogeneous expert architectures (the N =5 archetypes used for VoiceBank+DEMAND).\n966\tLeft: shared backbone with variable module slots. Right: example instantiations with architecturally distinct modules, each targeting a different noise-processing primitive (Home, Nature, Office,\n967\tTransport, Public). Built on this\u0001 compact set of canonical archetypes, sparse top-k=2 routing blends\n968\texperts per input to span the 52 =10-dimensional simplex of pairwise mixtures, yielding combinatorial coverage of the 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND without requiring a dedicated expert per\n969\tenvironment.\n970\t\n971\t14\n972\t\n973\t\fTable 2: Heterogeneous expert architectures used for VoiceBank+DEMAND. The notation GN(g)\n974\tdenotes group normalization with g groups; LN denotes layer normalization. Each architecture is\n975\tselected to match the inductive bias of its target scene family.\n976\tScene Category\n977\tHome (DKITCHEN, etc.)\n978\tNature (NPARK, etc.)\n979\tOffice (OMEETING, etc.)\n980\tTransport (TBUS, etc.)\n981\tPublic (PCAFETER, etc.)\n982\t\n983\tA.2\n984\t\n985\tArchitecture\n986\t513 → 1024 → GN(8) → 1024 → 513\n987\t513 → 2048 → 1024 → LN → 513\n988\t513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513\n989\t513 → 1536 → 1024 → 513\n990\t513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513\n991\t\n992\tDesign Motivation\n993\tLow-rank denoising for stationary tonal noise\n994\tWide receptive field for diffuse ambience\n995\tInformation bottleneck [54]\n996\tHarmonic basis expansion\n997\tGeneral-purpose universal approximator\n998\t\n999\tParams\n1000\t2.6M\n1001\t3.7M\n1002\t2.6M\n1003\t3.2M\n1004\t2.6M\n1005\t\n1006\tExpert Configurations for VoiceBank+DEMAND\n1007\t\n1008\tTable 2 lists the architectural specifications used in our VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments. Each\n1009\tarchetype maps to one of the major noise families in the dataset; we instantiate one expert per\n1010\tarchetype, yielding a compact yet diverse set of N = 5 processing primitives. The input dimension\n1011\tmatches the STFT frequency-bin count F = 513, and all experts produce outputs of the same\n1012\tdimension to maintain interface compatibility for routing.\n1013\tA.3\n1014\t\n1015\tArchetype Details\n1016\t\n1017\tHome expert. The kitchen, washing-machine, and living-room scenes share a common acoustic\n1018\tsignature: stationary tonal noise from appliances overlaid on relatively clean speech. We instantiate\n1019\ta compact two-layer expansion (513 → 1024) followed by group normalization with 8 groups,\n1020\twhich acts as a low-rank denoising operator. The narrow internal capacity is sufficient because the\n1021\tnoise subspace is approximately stationary, allowing the expert to suppress predictable tonal patterns\n1022\twithout overfitting.\n1023\tNature expert. Park, river, and field recordings contain diffuse, broadband ambient energy that fills\n1024\twide spectral regions. We employ a wider hidden layer (513 → 2048 → 1024) followed by layer\n1025\tnormalization to maintain stable activations across the expanded receptive field. This architecture\n1026\ttrades parameter count for the spectral coverage required to model ambient acoustic textures.\n1027\tOffice expert. Meeting and office scenes feature overlapping voices and structured background\n1028\tchatter. We adopt an information-bottleneck design [54] (513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513) that\n1029\texplicitly compresses to a 512-dimensional representation in the middle, encouraging the expert to\n1030\tretain only task-relevant information and suppress competing voiced sources.\n1031\tTransport expert. Bus, car, and metro noise is dominated by harmonic and quasi-periodic energy\n1032\tfrom engines, motors, and wheel-rail interactions. We use a wide-narrow-output structure (513 →\n1033\t1536 → 1024 → 513) without intermediate normalization, allowing the expert to fit a learned\n1034\tharmonic basis at the first layer and refine it before projection. Skipping internal normalization\n1035\tpreserves amplitude information critical for engine-noise spectral envelopes.\n1036\tPublic expert. Cafeteria, station, and restaurant scenes are the most challenging, combining nonstationary crowd noise, music, transient events, and reverberation. We adopt a symmetric architecture\n1037\twith a single LayerNorm in the middle (513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513), which serves as a\n1038\tuniversal approximator for the heterogeneous mixture without strong inductive bias. Sparse routing\n1039\tallows this general-purpose expert to be combined with archetype-specific experts when partial\n1040\tstructure is detectable in the input.\n1041\tA.4\n1042\t\n1043\tGeneralization to Other Datasets\n1044\t\n1045\tThe archetype set is intentionally modular: adding a new expert requires only conforming to the\n1046\tshared backbone interface (matching input/output dimensionality F ). The auxiliary load-balancing\n1047\tloss (Eq. 2) automatically integrates new experts into the routing distribution without retraining the\n1048\texisting ones from scratch, with brief router fine-tuning recommended. For deployments encountering\n1049\tnoise families not represented in the current set, for instance, reverberant cocktail-party recordings or\n1050\tindustrial machinery noise, a corresponding archetype can be added (e.g., a dereverberation-oriented\n1051\texpert with longer temporal context) without altering the rest of the framework.\n1052\t15\n1053\t\n1054\t\fTable 3: Loss weights and bound parameters used in all VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments.\n1055\tHyperparameter\n1056\t\n1057\tValue\n1058\t\n1059\tHyperparameter\n1060\t\n1061\tValue\n1062\t\n1063\tλSB (overall SB)\n1064\tλpath (path consistency)\n1065\tλtraj (trajectory)\n1066\tMmax (mask ceiling)\n1067\tϕmax (phase ceiling)\n1068\t\n1069\t1.0\n1070\t0.1\n1071\t0.05\n1072\t5.0\n1073\tπ/4\n1074\t\n1075\tλaux (load balance)\n1076\tλcal (calibration)\n1077\tλI (importance variance)\n1078\tλL (load variance)\n1079\t—\n1080\t\n1081\t0.01\n1082\t0.05\n1083\t0.5\n1084\t0.5\n1085\t—\n1086\t\n1087\tB\n1088\t\n1089\tU-Net and Training Details\n1090\t\n1091\tB.1\n1092\t\n1093\tWaveform-Domain U-Net Architecture\n1094\t\n1095\tThe denoiser x̂θ is a 1D U-Net with 4 encoder/decoder levels. Each encoder block applies a strided\n1096\t1D convolution (stride 2, kernel 7) followed by GroupNorm and SiLU activation, halving temporal\n1097\tresolution and doubling channels (64 → 128 → 256 → 512). The decoder mirrors this structure with\n1098\ttransposed convolutions and skip connections. A latent bottleneck operates at the lowest resolution to\n1099\taggregate global temporal context before decoding. The noisy observation y is concatenated with\n1100\txt along the channel dimension at the input. Timestep t is encoded via a sinusoidal embedding\n1101\t(dim 128) projected through a 2-layer MLP (256 → 512), and injected into every block via FiLM\n1102\tscale-and-shift conditioning. The aleatoric uncertainty head σθ2 is a separate 2-layer MLP attached to\n1103\tthe final decoder feature, producing per-sample log-variance.\n1104\tB.2\n1105\t\n1106\tLoss Weights\n1107\t\n1108\tThe full training objective combines six loss components (Lrec , Ldata\n1109\tSB , Lpath , Ltraj , Laux , Lcal )\n1110\twith the following weights, tuned via grid search on a 10% held-out validation split of VoiceBank+DEMAND, as shown in Table 3.\n1111\tB.3\n1112\t\n1113\tTraining Schedule\n1114\t\n1115\tWe use AdamW with β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.999, weight decay 0.01, and gradient clipping at norm 1.0.\n1116\tThe learning rate follows cosine annealing from 2 × 10−4 to 1 × 10−6 over 200 epochs, with a\n1117\t5-epoch linear warm-up. Batch size is 32 across 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (effective batch 64).\n1118\tMixed-precision (bfloat16) training is used throughout. Total wall-clock training time: approximately\n1119\t48 hours.\n1120\tB.4\n1121\t\n1122\tSB Sampling Configuration\n1123\t\n1124\t\u0001\n1125\tThe cumulative cosine schedule follows β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 (t/T + s)/(1 + s) · π/2\n1126\tand offset s = 0.008, yielding the per-step ratio βt = β̄t /β̄t−1 . We adopt the convention that\n1127\tβ̄t decreases monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0, which keeps Eq. 4 symmetric in x and y;\n1128\tthis assigns β̄t the role played by ᾱt in the standard DDPM convention. The bridge perturbation\n1129\tin both thep\n1130\tforward construction (Eq. 4) and the reverse update (Eq. 5) shares a single schedule\n1131\tσt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) with σmax = 0.05, which vanishes at both endpoints (σ0 = σT = 0) and\n1132\tmatches the boundary conditions x0 = x, xT = y. For inference, we use K = 8 sampling steps with\n1133\tthe non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ where γ = 0.6 (front-loaded).\n1134\tAt each training step we sample a single timestep t ∼ U [0, T ] and supervise x̂θ via Eq. 6, while\n1135\tLpath (Eq. 7) is evaluated on an independently sampled pair (t, t′ ) with independent perturbations\n1136\tϵ, ϵ′ drawn from Eq. 4. Although inference uses a K-step rollout while training optimizes singlestep predictions, Lpath explicitly enforces that x̂θ (xt , y, t) remains consistent across timesteps, so\n1137\tthe multi-step trajectory operates on predictions that the model has been trained to keep mutually\n1138\tagreeable. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj further anchors each per-step prediction to the forward\n1139\tbridge construction, yielding a reverse process that is stable under the discretization mismatch\n1140\tbetween training and inference.\n1141\t16\n1142\t\n1143\t\fB.5\n1144\t\n1145\tAlgorithm Details\n1146\t\n1147\tThe complete training procedure with explicit loss accumulation is given in Algorithm 1.\n1148\tAlgorithm 1 HybridSB-MoE end-to-end training (full version)\n1149\tN\n1150\tInput: Paired training set {(y (i) , x(i) )}M\n1151\ti=1 ; experts {Ei }i=1 ; router G; SB denoiser x̂θ ; fusion\n1152\tnetwork F ; loss weights {λ∗ }\n1153\tOutput: Trained parameters Θ\n1154\t1: for each minibatch B = {(y, x)} do\n1155\t2:\n1156\t// Spectral pathway\n1157\t3:\n1158\tCompute STFT: S{y} = STFT(y); extract z = log |S{y}|\n1159\t4:\n1160\tRoute: G(z) ← α Garch\n1161\tP (z) + (1 − α) Gtoken (z); select top-k indices Ik\n1162\t5:\n1163\tAggregate: x̂spec ← i∈Ik Gi (z) Ei (z)\n1164\t6:\n1165\tPredict M̂ , ∆ϕ via Eq. 3; reconstruct xspec via iSTFT\n1166\t7:\n1167\tCompute uepi (variance of top-k expert outputs)\n1168\t8:\n1169\t// Waveform pathway\n1170\tSample two independent timesteps t, t′ ∼ U [0, T ] and noises ϵ, ϵ′ ∼ N (0, I) // single Monte\n1171\t9:\n1172\tCarlo sample of Et,t′ per minibatch\n1173\tp\n1174\tp\n1175\t10:\n1176\tForm bridge states: xt ← p β̄t x + p1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ;\n1177\txt′ ← β̄t′ x + 1 − β̄t′ y + σt′ ϵ′\n1178\t11:\n1179\tPredict x̂θ (xt , y, t), x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ ), and aleatoric variance σθ2 at (xt , t)\n1180\t′ 2\n1181\tCompute Ldata\n1182\t12:\n1183\tSB (Eq. 6, on (xt , t)); Lpath = ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t )∥ (Eq. 7); Ltraj (Eq.\n1184\t8, on (xt , t))\n1185\t13:\n1186\tSet uale ← exp(σθ2 )t ; set xwave ← x̂θ (xt , y, t) as the current single-step prediction (full\n1187\tK-step rollout used at inference)\n1188\t14:\n1189\t// Fusion\n1190\t15:\n1191\tũepi , ũale ← z-norm(uepi , uale ) // running statistics\n1192\t16:\n1193\tw ← σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale ))\n1194\t17:\n1195\tx̂ ← w · xspec + (1 − w) · xwave\n1196\t18:\n1197\tCompute Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥22 , Laux (Eq. 2), Lcal (Eq. 11) // Lcal uses pre-norm uepi , uale\n1198\t19:\n1199\t// Joint update\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e71df7e2-cb51-4e95-971f-0cc9eee0f3dc", "uuid": "d9bc0aec-d607-4006-8589-864cace001ae", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T06:11:42.200Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "time-domain single-channel speech separation,” in ICASSP, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n[7] J. Chen, Z. Wang, D. Tuo, Z. Wu, S. Kang, and H. Meng, “Fullsubnet+: Channel attention\nfullsubnet with complex spectrograms for speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2022-2022 IEEE\nInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2022,\npp. 7857–7861.\n[8] Z.-Q. Wang, S. Cornell, S. Choi, Y. Lee, B.-Y. Kim, and S. Watanabe, “Tf-gridnet: Integrating\nfull- and sub-band modeling for speech separation,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech,\nand Language Processing, vol. 31, pp. 3221–3236, 2023.\n[9] J. Ho, A. Jain, and P. Abbeel, “Denoising diffusion probabilistic models,” in Proceedings of the\n34th International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, ser. NIPS ’20. Red\nHook, NY, USA: Curran Associates Inc., 2020.\n[10] S. Welker, J. Richter, and T. Gerkmann, “Speech enhancement with score-based generative\nmodels in the complex stft domain,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2203.17004, 2022.\n[11] J. Richter, S. Welker, J.-M. Lemercier, B. Lay, and T. Gerkmann, “Speech enhancement and\ndereverberation with diffusion-based generative models,” IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\nSpeech, and Language Processing, vol. 31, pp. 2351–2364, 2023.\n[12] A. Sivaraman and M. Kim, “Sparse mixture of local experts for efficient speech enhancement,”\narXiv preprint arXiv:2005.08128, 2020.\n[13] S. E. Chazan, J. Goldberger, and S. Gannot, “Speech enhancement with mixture of deep experts\nwith clean clustering pre-training,” in ICASSP 2021 - 2021 IEEE International Conference on\nAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2021, pp. 716–720.\n[14] S. Boll, “Suppression of acoustic noise in speech using spectral subtraction,” IEEE Transactions\non Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing, vol. 27, no. 2, pp. 113–120, 1979.\n[15] Y. Ephraim and D. Malah, “Speech enhancement using a minimum-mean square error short-time\nspectral amplitude estimator,” IEEE Transactions on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing,\nvol. 32, no. 6, pp. 1109–1121, 1984.\n[16] J. L. Roux, S. Wisdom, H. Erdogan, and J. R. Hershey, “Sdr – half-baked or well done?” in\nICASSP 2019 - 2019 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), 2019, pp. 626–630.\n[17] R. Chao, W.-H. Cheng, M. La Quatra, S. M. Siniscalchi, C.-H. H. Yang, S.-W. Fu, and Y. Tsao,\n“An investigation of incorporating mamba for speech enhancement,” in 2024 IEEE Spoken\nLanguage Technology Workshop (SLT). IEEE, 2024, pp. 302–308.\n10\n\n\f[18] J. Wang, Z. Lin, T. Wang, M. Ge, L. Wang, and J. Dang, “Mamba-seunet: Mamba unet for\nmonaural speech enhancement,” in ICASSP 2025 - 2025 IEEE International Conference on\nAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[19] H. Shi, K. Shimada, M. Hirano, T. Shibuya, Y. Koyama, Z. Zhong, S. Takahashi, T. Kawahara,\nand Y. Mitsufuji, “Diffusion-based speech enhancement with joint generative and predictive\ndecoders,” in ICASSP 2024 - 2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and\nSignal Processing (ICASSP), 2024, pp. 12 951–12 955.\n[20] S. T. Yousif and B. M. Mahmmod, “Speech enhancement algorithms: A systematic literature\nreview,” Algorithms, vol. 18, no. 5, p. 272, 2025.\n[21] T. Chen, G.-H. Liu, and E. A. Theodorou, “Likelihood training of schrödinger bridge using\nforward–backward sdes theory,” in ICLR, 2022.\n[22] Y. Song, J. Sohl-Dickstein, D. P. Kingma, A. Kumar, S. Ermon, and B. Poole, “Score-based\ngenerative modeling through stochastic differential equations,” in International Conference\non Learning Representations, 2021. [Online]. Available: https://openreview.net/forum?id=\nPxTIG12RRHS\n[23] J. Song, C. Meng, and S. Ermon, “Denoising diffusion implicit models,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2010.02502, 2020.\n[24] Y. Lipman, R. T. Q. Chen, H. Ben-Hamu, M. Nickel, and M. Le, “Flow matching for generative\nmodeling,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n[25] X. Liu, C. Gong, and Q. Liu, “Flow straight and fast: Learning to generate and transfer data\nwith rectified flow,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n[26] V. De Bortoli, J. Thornton, J. Heng, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion Schrödinger bridge with\napplications to score-based generative modeling,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing\nSystems, vol. 34, 2021, pp. 17 695–17 709.\n[27] Y. Shi, V. De Bortoli, A. Campbell, and A. Doucet, “Diffusion schrödinger bridge matching,”\nin Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, A. Oh, T. Naumann, A. Globerson,\nK. Saenko, M. Hardt, and S. Levine, Eds., vol. 36. Curran Associates, Inc., 2023, pp.\n62 183–62 223. [Online]. Available: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/\nc428adf74782c2092d254329b6b02482-Paper-Conference.pdf\n[28] A. Jukić, R. Korostik, J. Balam, and B. Ginsburg, “Schrödinger bridge for generative speech\nenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.16074, 2024.\n[29] S. Wang, S. Liu, A. Harper, P. Kendrick, M. Salzmann, and M. Cernak, “Diffusion-based\nspeech enhancement with Schrödinger bridge and symmetric noise schedule,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2409.05116, 2024.\n[30] Z. Tang, T. Hang, S. Gu, D. Chen, and B. Guo, “Simplified diffusion schrödinger bridge,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2403.14623, 2024.\n[31] J. Yang, S. Wang, C. Wu, L. Guo, and F. Fan, “Schrödinger bridge mamba for one-step speech\nenhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.16834, 2025.\n[32] H. Zhang, G. Li, P. Wu, Y. Gao, and H. Zhang, “Sb-senet: Diffusion model based on schrödinger\nbridge for speech enhancement,” Applied Acoustics, vol. 236, p. 110742, 2025.\n[33] L. Xu, L. F. Yan, and W. B. Kleijn, “Robust one-step speech enhancement via consistency\ndistillation,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.05688, 2025.\n[34] Y. Song, P. Dhariwal, M. Chen, and I. Sutskever, “Consistency models,” in International\nConference on Machine Learning, 2023.\n[35] S. Han, S. Lee, J. Lee, and K. Lee, “Few-step adversarial schrödinger bridge for generative\nspeech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.01460, 2025.\n11\n\n\f[36] S. Nishigori, K. Saito, N. Murata, M. Hirano, S. Takahashi, and Y. Mitsufuji,\n“Schrödinger bridge consistency trajectory models for speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2507.11925, 2025.\n[37] S. Chen, S. Chewi, J. Li, Y. Li, A. Salim, and A. R. Zhang, “Sampling is as easy as learning the\nscore: theory for diffusion models with minimal data assumptions,” in International Conference\non Learning Representations, 2023.\n[38] N. Shazeer, A. Mirhoseini, K. Maziarz, A. Davis, Q. Le, G. Hinton, and J. Dean, “Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:1701.06538, 2017.\n[39] W. Fedus, B. Zoph, and N. Shazeer, “Switch transformers: Scaling to trillion parameter models\nwith simple and efficient sparsity,” Journal of Machine Learning Research, vol. 23, no. 120, pp.\n1–39, 2022. [Online]. Available: http://jmlr.org/papers/v23/21-0998.html\n[40] D. Lepikhin, H. Lee, Y. Xu, D. Chen, O. Firat, Y. Huang, M. Krikun, N. Shazeer, and Z. Chen,\n“Gshard: Scaling giant models with conditional computation and automatic sharding,” arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2006.16668, 2020.\n[41] A. Sivaraman and M. Kim, “Zero-shot personalized speech enhancement through speakerinformed model selection,” in 2021 IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to\nAudio and Acoustics (WASPAA), 2021, pp. 171–175.\n[42] R. Miccini, M. Kim, C. Laroche, L. Pezzarossa, and P. Smaragdis, “Adaptive slimming for\nscalable and efficient speech enhancement,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.04879, 2025.\n[43] B. Lakshminarayanan, A. Pritzel, and C. Blundell, “Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty\nestimation using deep ensembles,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems,\nvol. 30, 2017.\n[44] C. Guo, G. Pleiss, Y. Sun, and K. Q. Weinberger, “On calibration of modern neural networks,”\nin Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Machine Learning - Volume 70, ser.\nICML’17. JMLR.org, 2017, p. 1321–1330.\n[45] J. B. Allen and L. R. Rabiner, “A unified approach to short-time fourier analysis and synthesis,”\nProceedings of the IEEE, vol. 65, no. 11, pp. 1558–1564, 1977.\n[46] J. Thiemann, N. Ito, and E. Vincent, “The diverse environments multi-channel acoustic\nnoise database (demand): A database of multichannel environmental noise recordings,”\nProceedings of Meetings on Acoustics, vol. 19, no. 1, p. 035081, 05 2013. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://doi.org/10.1121/1.4799597\n[47] Y. Wu and K. He, “Group normalization,” in Proceedings of the European Conference on\nComputer Vision, 2018, pp. 3–19.\n[48] G. Peyre and M. Cuturi, “Computational optimal transport,” Foundations and Trends in Machine\nLearning, vol. 11, no. 5-6, pp. 355–607, 2019.\n[49] A. Nichol and P. Dhariwal, “Improved denoising diffusion probabilistic models,” 2021.\n[Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09672\n[50] C. Valentini-Botinhao, X. Wang, S. Takaki, and J. Yamagishi, “Investigating rnn-based speech\nenhancement methods for noise-robust text-to-speech,” in 9th ISCA Workshop on Speech\nSynthesis Workshop (SSW 9), 2016, pp. 146–152.\n[51] A. Rix, J. Beerends, M. Hollier, and A. Hekstra, “Perceptual evaluation of speech quality\n(pesq)-a new method for speech quality assessment of telephone networks and codecs,” in 2001\nIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing. Proceedings (Cat.\nNo.01CH37221), vol. 2, 2001, pp. 749–752 vol.2.\n[52] C. H. Taal, R. C. Hendriks, R. Heusdens, and J. Jensen, “An algorithm for intelligibility\nprediction of time–frequency weighted noisy speech,” IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech,\nand Language Processing, vol. 19, no. 7, pp. 2125–2136, 2011.\n12\n\n\f[53] Y. Hu and P. C. Loizou, “Evaluation of objective quality measures for speech enhancement,”\nIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 229–238,\n2008.\n[54] N. Tishby and N. Zaslavsky, “Deep learning and the information bottleneck principle,” in 2015\nIEEE Information Theory Workshop (ITW), 2015, pp. 1–5.\n\n13\n\n\fA\n\nHeterogeneous Expert Architectures\n\nThis appendix details the heterogeneous MoE layer described in Section 3 of the main paper. We\ndescribe the design philosophy that connects each architectural archetype to a canonical noise family,\nlist the configurations used for VoiceBank+DEMAND, and discuss how the archetype set generalizes\nto other datasets.\nA.1\n\nDesign Philosophy\n\nThe heterogeneous MoE is grounded in the empirical observation that real-world acoustic noise,\ndespite its surface diversity, can be decomposed into a small number of canonical processing primitives. Stationary tonal noise (e.g., kitchen appliances, refrigerator hum) is well-modeled by compact\nnormalized layers with low-rank structure; ambient natural backgrounds (e.g., park, river, field)\nbenefit from wide receptive fields with strong normalization to capture diffuse spectral content; office\nand meeting environments contain overlapping voices and structured interference best handled by\ninformation bottlenecks [54]; transportation noise (bus, car, metro) exhibits strong harmonic and\nquasi-periodic energy from engines and motors, naturally captured by harmonic basis expansion; and\nunstructured public-space mixtures (cafeteria, station, restaurant) require universal-approximationstyle general-purpose blocks. Rather than scaling capacity by replicating one architecture (as in\nhomogeneous MoE), we instantiate one expert per archetype, then rely on sparse routing to combine\nthem per input.\nFigure 4 (reproduced here for completeness) illustrates the shared backbone and the architecturally\ndistinct instantiations. All experts pass features through LayerNorm → variable internal modules\n→ bottleneck projection, ensuring interface compatibility for routing while permitting structurally\ndivergent computation in the middle.\n\nFigure 4: Heterogeneous expert architectures (the N =5 archetypes used for VoiceBank+DEMAND).\nLeft: shared backbone with variable module slots. Right: example instantiations with architecturally distinct modules, each targeting a different noise-processing primitive (Home, Nature, Office,\nTransport, Public). Built on this\u0001 compact set of canonical archetypes, sparse top-k=2 routing blends\nexperts per input to span the 52 =10-dimensional simplex of pairwise mixtures, yielding combinatorial coverage of the 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND without requiring a dedicated expert per\nenvironment.\n\n14\n\n\fTable 2: Heterogeneous expert architectures used for VoiceBank+DEMAND. The notation GN(g)\ndenotes group normalization with g groups; LN denotes layer normalization. Each architecture is\nselected to match the inductive bias of its target scene family.\nScene Category\nHome (DKITCHEN, etc.)\nNature (NPARK, etc.)\nOffice (OMEETING, etc.)\nTransport (TBUS, etc.)\nPublic (PCAFETER, etc.)\n\nA.2\n\nArchitecture\n513 → 1024 → GN(8) → 1024 → 513\n513 → 2048 → 1024 → LN → 513\n513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513\n513 → 1536 → 1024 → 513\n513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513\n\nDesign Motivation\nLow-rank denoising for stationary tonal noise\nWide receptive field for diffuse ambience\nInformation bottleneck [54]\nHarmonic basis expansion\nGeneral-purpose universal approximator\n\nParams\n2.6M\n3.7M\n2.6M\n3.2M\n2.6M\n\nExpert Configurations for VoiceBank+DEMAND\n\nTable 2 lists the architectural specifications used in our VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments. Each\narchetype maps to one of the major noise families in the dataset; we instantiate one expert per\narchetype, yielding a compact yet diverse set of N = 5 processing primitives. The input dimension\nmatches the STFT frequency-bin count F = 513, and all experts produce outputs of the same\ndimension to maintain interface compatibility for routing.\nA.3\n\nArchetype Details\n\nHome expert. The kitchen, washing-machine, and living-room scenes share a common acoustic\nsignature: stationary tonal noise from appliances overlaid on relatively clean speech. We instantiate\na compact two-layer expansion (513 → 1024) followed by group normalization with 8 groups,\nwhich acts as a low-rank denoising operator. The narrow internal capacity is sufficient because the\nnoise subspace is approximately stationary, allowing the expert to suppress predictable tonal patterns\nwithout overfitting.\nNature expert. Park, river, and field recordings contain diffuse, broadband ambient energy that fills\nwide spectral regions. We employ a wider hidden layer (513 → 2048 → 1024) followed by layer\nnormalization to maintain stable activations across the expanded receptive field. This architecture\ntrades parameter count for the spectral coverage required to model ambient acoustic textures.\nOffice expert. Meeting and office scenes feature overlapping voices and structured background\nchatter. We adopt an information-bottleneck design [54] (513 → 1024 → 512 → 1024 → 513) that\nexplicitly compresses to a 512-dimensional representation in the middle, encouraging the expert to\nretain only task-relevant information and suppress competing voiced sources.\nTransport expert. Bus, car, and metro noise is dominated by harmonic and quasi-periodic energy\nfrom engines, motors, and wheel-rail interactions. We use a wide-narrow-output structure (513 →\n1536 → 1024 → 513) without intermediate normalization, allowing the expert to fit a learned\nharmonic basis at the first layer and refine it before projection. Skipping internal normalization\npreserves amplitude information critical for engine-noise spectral envelopes.\nPublic expert. Cafeteria, station, and restaurant scenes are the most challenging, combining nonstationary crowd noise, music, transient events, and reverberation. We adopt a symmetric architecture\nwith a single LayerNorm in the middle (513 → 1024 → LN → 1024 → 513), which serves as a\nuniversal approximator for the heterogeneous mixture without strong inductive bias. Sparse routing\nallows this general-purpose expert to be combined with archetype-specific experts when partial\nstructure is detectable in the input.\nA.4\n\nGeneralization to Other Datasets\n\nThe archetype set is intentionally modular: adding a new expert requires only conforming to the\nshared backbone interface (matching input/output dimensionality F ). The auxiliary load-balancing\nloss (Eq. 2) automatically integrates new experts into the routing distribution without retraining the\nexisting ones from scratch, with brief router fine-tuning recommended. For deployments encountering\nnoise families not represented in the current set, for instance, reverberant cocktail-party recordings or\nindustrial machinery noise, a corresponding archetype can be added (e.g., a dereverberation-oriented\nexpert with longer temporal context) without altering the rest of the framework.\n15\n\n\fTable 3: Loss weights and bound parameters used in all VoiceBank+DEMAND experiments.\nHyperparameter\n\nValue\n\nHyperparameter\n\nValue\n\nλSB (overall SB)\nλpath (path consistency)\nλtraj (trajectory)\nMmax (mask ceiling)\nϕmax (phase ceiling)\n\n1.0\n0.1\n0.05\n5.0\nπ/4\n\nλaux (load balance)\nλcal (calibration)\nλI (importance variance)\nλL (load variance)\n—\n\n0.01\n0.05\n0.5\n0.5\n—\n\nB\n\nU-Net and Training Details\n\nB.1\n\nWaveform-Domain U-Net Architecture\n\nThe denoiser x̂θ is a 1D U-Net with 4 encoder/decoder levels. Each encoder block applies a strided\n1D convolution (stride 2, kernel 7) followed by GroupNorm and SiLU activation, halving temporal\nresolution and doubling channels (64 → 128 → 256 → 512). The decoder mirrors this structure with\ntransposed convolutions and skip connections. A latent bottleneck operates at the lowest resolution to\naggregate global temporal context before decoding. The noisy observation y is concatenated with\nxt along the channel dimension at the input. Timestep t is encoded via a sinusoidal embedding\n(dim 128) projected through a 2-layer MLP (256 → 512), and injected into every block via FiLM\nscale-and-shift conditioning. The aleatoric uncertainty head σθ2 is a separate 2-layer MLP attached to\nthe final decoder feature, producing per-sample log-variance.\nB.2\n\nLoss Weights\n\nThe full training objective combines six loss components (Lrec , Ldata\nSB , Lpath , Ltraj , Laux , Lcal )\nwith the following weights, tuned via grid search on a 10% held-out validation split of VoiceBank+DEMAND, as shown in Table 3.\nB.3\n\nTraining Schedule\n\nWe use AdamW with β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.999, weight decay 0.01, and gradient clipping at norm 1.0.\nThe learning rate follows cosine annealing from 2 × 10−4 to 1 × 10−6 over 200 epochs, with a\n5-epoch linear warm-up. Batch size is 32 across 2 NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (effective batch 64).\nMixed-precision (bfloat16) training is used throughout. Total wall-clock training time: approximately\n48 hours.\nB.4\n\nSB Sampling Configuration\n\n\u0001\nThe cumulative cosine schedule follows β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 (t/T + s)/(1 + s) · π/2\nand offset s = 0.008, yielding the per-step ratio βt = β̄t /β̄t−1 . We adopt the convention that\nβ̄t decreases monotonically from β̄0 = 1 to β̄T = 0, which keeps Eq. 4 symmetric in x and y;\nthis assigns β̄t the role played by ᾱt in the standard DDPM convention. The bridge perturbation\nin both thep\nforward construction (Eq. 4) and the reverse update (Eq. 5) shares a single schedule\nσt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t ) with σmax = 0.05, which vanishes at both endpoints (σ0 = σT = 0) and\nmatches the boundary conditions x0 = x, xT = y. For inference, we use K = 8 sampling steps with\nthe non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ where γ = 0.6 (front-loaded).\nAt each training step we sample a single timestep t ∼ U [0, T ] and supervise x̂θ via Eq. 6, while\nLpath (Eq. 7) is evaluated on an independently sampled pair (t, t′ ) with independent perturbations\nϵ, ϵ′ drawn from Eq. 4. Although inference uses a K-step rollout while training optimizes singlestep predictions, Lpath explicitly enforces that x̂θ (xt , y, t) remains consistent across timesteps, so\nthe multi-step trajectory operates on predictions that the model has been trained to keep mutually\nagreeable. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj further anchors each per-step prediction to the forward\nbridge construction, yielding a reverse process that is stable under the discretization mismatch\nbetween training and inference.\n16\n\n\fB.5\n\nAlgorithm Details\n\nThe complete training procedure with explicit loss accumulation is given in Algorithm 1.\nAlgorithm 1 HybridSB-MoE end-to-end training (full version)\nN\nInput: Paired training set {(y (i) , x(i) )}M\ni=1 ; experts {Ei }i=1 ; router G; SB denoiser x̂θ ; fusion\nnetwork F ; loss weights {λ∗ }\nOutput: Trained parameters Θ\n1: for each minibatch B = {(y, x)} do\n2:\n// Spectral pathway\n3:\nCompute STFT: S{y} = STFT(y); extract z = log |S{y}|\n4:\nRoute: G(z) ← α Garch\nP (z) + (1 − α) Gtoken (z); select top-k indices Ik\n5:\nAggregate: x̂spec ← i∈Ik Gi (z) Ei (z)\n6:\nPredict M̂ , ∆ϕ via Eq. 3; reconstruct xspec via iSTFT\n7:\nCompute uepi (variance of top-k expert outputs)\n8:\n// Waveform pathway\nSample two independent timesteps t, t′ ∼ U [0, T ] and noises ϵ, ϵ′ ∼ N (0, I) // single Monte\n9:\nCarlo sample of Et,t′ per minibatch\np\np\n10:\nForm bridge states: xt ← p β̄t x + p1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ;\nxt′ ← β̄t′ x + 1 − β̄t′ y + σt′ ϵ′\n11:\nPredict x̂θ (xt , y, t), x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ ), and aleatoric variance σθ2 at (xt , t)\n′ 2\nCompute Ldata\n12:\nSB (Eq. 6, on (xt , t)); Lpath = ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t )∥ (Eq. 7); Ltraj (Eq.\n8, on (xt , t))\n13:\nSet uale ← exp(σθ2 )t ; set xwave ← x̂θ (xt , y, t) as the current single-step prediction (full\nK-step rollout used at inference)\n14:\n// Fusion\n15:\nũepi , ũale ← z-norm(uepi , uale ) // running statistics\n16:\nw ← σ(MLP(ũepi , ũale ))\n17:\nx̂ ← w · xspec + (1 − w) · xwave\n18:\nCompute Lrec = ∥x̂ − x∥22 , Laux (Eq. 2), Lcal (Eq. 11) // Lcal uses pre-norm uepi , uale\n19:\n// Joint update", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 800, "totalLines": 1604}}}
