{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-93a16a491a6416a9", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t20:\n1201\tLSB ← Ldata\n1202\tSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj\n1203\t21:\n1204\tL ← Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal\n1205\t22:\n1206\tUpdate Θ ← Θ − η∇Θ L\n1207\t23: end for\n1208\t\n1209\tC\n1210\t\n1211\tProof of Theorem 1\n1212\t\n1213\tWe prove the K-step discretization bound stated in Section 3 (Theorem 1) of the main paper. The\n1214\tstrategy is to decompose the W2 distance into (i) a one-step model-fidelity error driven by L⋆path and\n1215\tL⋆traj , and (ii) a Riemann-sum discretization error in K controlled by the schedule’s front-loading\n1216\texponent γ, then to combine the two via a synchronous coupling and a triangle inequality. The\n1217\targument adapts standard SDE-discretization analysis (cf. [37, 26]) to the doubly-conditioned bridge\n1218\tprocess of Eq. (4–5).\n1219\tC.1\n1220\t\n1221\tSetup and Notation\n1222\t\n1223\tFix a noisy observation y and let {xt }t∈[0,T ] be the true continuous-time bridge process from\n1224\tclean speech x to y, so that pbr\n1225\tt (· | y) is its time-t marginal. Let x̂θ be the trained denoiser,\n1226\tand let p̂K be the law at t=0 of the K-step rollout produced by Eq. (5) initialized at xT = y\n1227\tγ\n1228\talong the non-uniform schedule\n1229\t{tk }K\n1230\tk=0 with tK = T and tk = T (k/K) , γ ∈ (0, 1]. We use\n1231\tR\n1232\t2\n1233\t2\n1234\tW2 (µ, ν) = inf π∈Γ(µ,ν) ∥u − v∥ dπ(u, v) in the standard definition.\n1235\tThroughout, C denotes a generic constant depending only on the regularity constants of Assumption\n1236\t1; its value may change line by line.\n1237\t17\n1238\t\n1239\t\fC.2\n1240\t\n1241\tOne-step Model-fidelity Lemma\n1242\t\n1243\tLemma 1 (Model-fidelity error). Under Assumption 1, for each step tk → tk−1 of Eq. (5), the\n1244\tconditional one-step error\n1245\th\n1246\ti\n1247\t2\n1248\tδk := E x̂θ (xtk , y, tk ) − x\n1249\txtk , y\n1250\tsatisfies E[δk ] ≤ C (L⋆path + L⋆traj ) uniformly in k, where the expectation on the right is over the\n1251\tbridge construction Eq. (4).\n1252\tProof. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj of Eq. (8), evaluated at the optimum, satisfies\n1253\t\u0015\n1254\t\u0014\n1255\tq\n1256\tq\n1257\t2\n1258\t= L⋆traj .\n1259\tE xt − ( β̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y)\n1260\tp\n1261\tp\n1262\tSubstituting the forward construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ from Eq. (4) and rearranging,\n1263\tq\n1264\t\u0002\n1265\t\u0003\n1266\tβ̄t E ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 − 2 β̄t σt E[⟨x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x, ϵ⟩] + σt2 E∥ϵ∥2 = L⋆traj .\n1267\tBy\n1268\tand E∥ϵ∥2 = d (the signal dimension), the cross term is bounded by\n1269\tp\n1270\t√ Cauchy–Schwarz\n1271\t2\n1272\t2 dσt E∥x̂θ − x∥ , and bounded marginals (Assumption 1(iii)) yield\n1273\t\u0002\n1274\t\u0003\n1275\t\u0001\n1276\t1 ⋆\n1277\t2\n1278\tE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 ≤\n1279\tLtraj + Cσt2 ≤ C(L⋆traj + σmax\n1280\t),\n1281\tβ̄t\n1282\t\n1283\t(13)\n1284\t\n1285\ton [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] for any fixed ϵ0 > 0, since β̄t is bounded away from zero on this set; near the\n1286\tboundaries, the schedule’s vanishing σt controls the residual.\n1287\tThe path-consistency loss Lpath of Eq. (7) controls the deviation of x̂θ across timesteps:\n1288\t\u0002\n1289\t\u0003\n1290\tE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥2 ≤ 2L⋆path .\n1291\t\n1292\t(14)\n1293\t\n1294\tCombining Eq. (13) with Eq. (14) and the triangle inequality on the implied error gives the claimed\n1295\tbound on E[δk ].\n1296\tC.3\n1297\t\n1298\tDiscretization Lemma\n1299\t\n1300\tp\n1301\tp\n1302\tLemma 2 (Riemann discretization rate). Let Φ(t) = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y denote the deterministic\n1303\tcomponent of the bridge state at time t (Eq. 4 with σt ϵ removed). Under Assumption 1, the K-step\n1304\tRiemann approximation ΦK of Φ along the non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\n1305\tsatisfies\n1306\tsup Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 ) ≤ C K − min(1,γ) .\n1307\tk\n1308\t\n1309\tProof. Standard mean-value estimates on C 1 schedules yield local error |Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 )| ≤\n1310\tC (∆tk ) ∥Φ′ ∥∞ on [tk−1 , tk ], where ∆tk = tk − tk−1 . For the schedule tk = T (k/K)γ ,\n1311\t\u0001\n1312\t∆tk = T (k/K)γ − ((k − 1)/K)γ ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 .\n1313\tFor γ ≤ 1, k γ−1 is non-increasing in k, so supk ∆tk = ∆t1 ≤ T K −γ , giving the stated bound with\n1314\texponent min(1, γ) = γ.\n1315\tRemark. Front-loading (γ<1) does not improve the worst-case rate exponent. However, since\n1316\t∆tk ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 is non-increasing in k for γ ≤ 1, the schedule concentrates many small\n1317\tsteps near tK = T , where the reverse rollout of Eq. 5 is initialized at xT = y and where per-step\n1318\terrors propagate through all subsequent reverse-sampling iterations. The single large step ∆t1 , by\n1319\tcontrast, traverses the low-t regime in which xt already lies close to x and x̂θ is most accurate.\n1320\tThis redistribution—not the asymptotic rate—is what empirically dominates small-K reconstruction\n1321\tquality.\n1322\t18\n1323\t\n1324\t\fC.4\n1325\t\n1326\tCombining the Two Sources of Error\n1327\t\n1328\tLet x̂(K) denote the random variable whose law is p̂K , and let X ∼ pbr\n1329\t0 . We construct a synchronous\n1330\tcoupling between x̂(K) and X by sharing the noise variables z across the K rollout steps with the\n1331\tcorresponding bridge perturbation ϵ in the continuous process. Under this coupling,\n1332\tK\n1333\tK\n1334\th\n1335\ti\n1336\tX\n1337\tY\n1338\tX\n1339\t\u00012\n1340\t(K)\n1341\t2\n1342\t2\n1343\tW22 (p̂K , pbr\n1344\t)\n1345\t≤\n1346\tE\n1347\t∥x̂\n1348\t−\n1349\tX∥\n1350\t≤\n1351\t2\n1352\t∆t\n1353\tE[δ\n1354\t]\n1355\t·\n1356\t(1\n1357\t+\n1358\tL\n1359\t∆t\n1360\t)\n1361\t+\n1362\t2\n1363\t∆tk · disck ,\n1364\tk\n1365\tk\n1366\tx\n1367\tj\n1368\t0\n1369\tk=1\n1370\t\n1371\tj<k\n1372\t\n1373\tk=1\n1374\t\n1375\twhere disck is the Riemann error from Lemma 2; the ∆tk weighting on the model-fidelity term tracks\n1376\tthe time-averaged definition of L⋆traj , L⋆path (Eqs. 8,7), so each step contributes proportionally to its\n1377\tQ\n1378\ttime interval. The Lipschitz factor j (1 + Lx ∆tj ) is uniformly bounded by exp(Lx T ), absorbing it\n1379\tP\n1380\tinto the constant. By Lemma 1 and k ∆tk = T , the first sum is bounded by C T (L⋆path + L⋆traj ),\n1381\tP\n1382\tindependent of K. By Lemma 2 together with k (∆tk )2 ≤ T supk ∆tk = T 2 K −γ ,√the second\n1383\t√\n1384\t√\n1385\tsum is bounded by C K −2 min(1,γ) . Taking square roots and applying a + b ≤ a + b yields\n1386\tq\n1387\t−α\n1388\tW2 (p̂K , pbr\n1389\t+ C2 L⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n1390\t0 ) ≤ C1 K\n1391\twhich is exactly Eq. (9).\n1392\tC.5\n1393\t\n1394\tJustification of Assumption 1\n1395\t\n1396\tWe address each of the three regularity conditions in turn and explain why each is reasonable in our\n1397\tsetting.\n1398\t(i) Lipschitz continuity of x̂θ . The condition that x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and\n1399\tLt -Lipschitz in t is standard in diffusion-sampling analyses [37, 26]. It is justified architecturally\n1400\tand empirically: (a) our denoiser is a 1D U-Net with GroupNorm [47] and SiLU activations, both\n1401\tLipschitz; (b) the convolutional layers’ Lipschitz constants are bounded since weights remain finite\n1402\tunder our AdamW training with weight decay 0.01 and gradient clipping at norm 1.0 (Appendix B);\n1403\t(c) timestep dependence enters only through a smooth sinusoidal embedding followed by FiLM\n1404\tconditioning, which is C ∞ in t and so trivially Lipschitz on the compact interval [0, T ]. We do\n1405\tnot enforce a target Lx explicitly (e.g., via spectral normalization); the assumption asserts that the\n1406\ttrained network is Lipschitz with some finite constant, which is automatic for any network without\n1407\tunbounded activations or singular layers. Tighter constant estimates—needed only for quantitatively\n1408\ttight versions of the bound—would require spectral norm tracking and are left to future work.\n1409\t(ii) Smoothness of the schedules. The cosine schedule β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +\n1410\ts)/(1 + s) · π/2) is in fact C ∞ on [0, T ], considerably stronger than the C 1 requirement: it is the\n1411\tcomposition of a smooth trigonometric function with an affine reparameterization. Its monotonicity\n1412\tfollows from cos2 being strictly decreasing on the relevant subinterval, and its first and second\n1413\tderivatives are uniformly bounded on [0, T ] since the schedule arguments stay in a compact\n1414\tp subset of\n1415\t[0, π/2] where cos and sin are bounded. The bridge-perturbation magnitude σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\n1416\tis C ∞ on the open interval (0, T ) and continuous on [0, T ] with σ0 = σT = 0; the square-root\n1417\tintroduces a one-sided Hölder singularity exactly at the endpoints, but standard SDE-discretization\n1418\targuments (e.g., [37]) accommodate this via a small endpoint truncation [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] that vanishes in\n1419\tthe bound’s leading order. In our setting all sampling steps lie strictly in the interior of [0, T ] with\n1420\ttK = T at the noisy endpoint where the bridge is deterministic, so endpoint singularities do not affect\n1421\tthe discretization.\n1422\t(iii) Bounded second moments.\n1423\tE∥xt ∥2 < ∞ uniformly in t follows directly from\n1424\tp The condition\n1425\tp\n1426\tthe bridge construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ: by Jensen’s inequality and the triangle\n1427\tinequality,\n1428\t2\n1429\tE∥xt ∥2 ≤ 3E∥x∥2 + 3E∥y∥2 + 3σmax\n1430\td,\n1431\twhere d is the signal dimension and the right-hand side is finite because audio waveforms are bounded\n1432\t(∥x∥∞ , ∥y∥∞ ≤ 1 after our standard amplitude normalization, so E∥x∥2 ≤ d and similarly for y).\n1433\tFor E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 , the Lipschitz condition (i) gives ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥ ≤ Lx ∥xt ∥ + |x̂θ (0, y, t)|, so\n1434\tfiniteness of the second moment of x̂θ follows from finiteness of E∥xt ∥2 together with boundedness\n1435\tof x̂θ at the reference input xt = 0 (a finite-norm constant for any fixed trained network on a fixed\n1436\t19\n1437\t\n1438\t\fPESQ\n1439\t\n1440\tSTOI (%)\n1441\t\n1442\tDomestic\n1443\t\n1444\tNatural\n1445\t\n1446\tOffice\n1447\t\n1448\tPublic\n1449\t\n1450\tTransportation\n1451\t\n1452\t96.6\n1453\t3.92\n1454\t\n1455\tPESQ\n1456\t\n1457\t95.8\n1458\t\n1459\t3.84\n1460\t\n1461\t3.80\n1462\t\n1463\tSTOI (%)\n1464\t\n1465\t96.2\n1466\t\n1467\t3.88\n1468\t\n1469\tPublic\n1470\t\n1471\tUS\n1472\tTB\n1473\t\n1474\tTC\n1475\tAR\n1476\t\n1477\tO\n1478\tET\n1479\tR\n1480\tTM\n1481\t\n1482\tER\n1483\tET\n1484\tPC\n1485\tAF\n1486\t\n1487\tN\n1488\t\n1489\tES\n1490\tTO\n1491\tPR\n1492\t\n1493\tG\n1494\t\n1495\tOffice\n1496\t\n1497\tPS\n1498\tTAT\n1499\tIO\n1500\t\n1501\tTIN\n1502\tEE\n1503\t\n1504\tICE\n1505\t\n1506\tNatural\n1507\t\n1508\tOM\n1509\t\n1510\tFF\n1511\tOO\n1512\t\n1513\tNF\n1514\t\n1515\tIEL\n1516\t\n1517\tD\n1518\t\n1519\tK\n1520\tNP\n1521\tAR\n1522\t\n1523\tIVE\n1524\tR\n1525\t\n1526\tN\n1527\t\n1528\tDomestic\n1529\t\n1530\tNR\n1531\t\n1532\tITC\n1533\tHE\n1534\tDK\n1535\t\n1536\tNG\n1537\tIVI\n1538\tDL\n1539\t\n1540\tDW\n1541\tAS\n1542\tHIN\n1543\t\n1544\tG\n1545\t\n1546\t95.4\n1547\t\n1548\tTransportation\n1549\t\n1550\tFigure 5: Scene-stratified performance across all 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND. Inner\n1551\tring: PESQ; outer ring: STOI (%). Colors indicate scene categories: Domestic (blue), Nature (green),\n1552\tOffice (orange), Public (red), Transport (purple). For reference, the strongest baseline ROSE-CD\n1553\t[33] achieves a mean PESQ of 3.85 across these conditions; our method exceeds this in every scene,\n1554\twith PESQ ranging from 3.84 to 3.92 (mean 3.88, standard deviation <0.03 across scenes). Y-axis\n1555\tranges are zoomed for visibility of cross-scene variation.\n1556\tobservation y). Both moments are therefore bounded by a constant depending only on Lx , σmax , d,\n1557\tand the amplitude bound on the data.\n1558\tIn summary, Assumption 1 encodes mild structural properties that hold automatically for our cosinescheduled SB construction with a standard U-Net denoiser trained on amplitude-normalized waveforms. The assumption fails only in pathological regimes (unbounded data, divergent network outputs,\n1559\tor non-smooth schedules) that are not relevant to our setting or to standard SE practice.\n1560\tC.6\n1561\t\n1562\tDiscussion\n1563\t\n1564\tA few caveats deserve mention. First, Assumption 1(i) is a Lipschitz condition on the trained network;\n1565\tit can be enforced via spectral normalization in practice, but in our experiments it is assumed rather\n1566\tthan explicitly imposed. Second, the bound is presented as a design-justifying inequality; it is loose\n1567\tin the sense that the constants C1 , C2 involve worst-case Lipschitz factors and are not numerically\n1568\testimated here. A tighter quantitative match to empirical PESQ-vs-K would require finer Lipschitz\n1569\testimates on the U-Net and is left for future work. Third, the bound shows that small K is justified\n1570\tconditional on the regularizers reaching small training-time values; if a model is trained without these\n1571\tterms, the second term of Eq. (9) does not vanish and the bound cannot guarantee small-K quality.\n1572\tThis is consistent with the role we ascribe to Lpath + Ltraj in the main paper.\n1573\t\n1574\tD\n1575\t\n1576\tScene-Stratified Performance\n1577\t\n1578\tFigure 5 breaks down enhancement performance across all 14 noise environments. Natural environments (NPARK, NRIVER, NFIELD) achieve the highest scores due to relatively stable spectral\n1579\tcharacteristics, while public spaces (PCAFETER, PSTATION, PRESTO) are slightly more challenging due to non-stationary crowd noise and unpredictable transient events. Despite this variation, the\n1580\tspread is small (PESQ standard deviation <0.03), indicating that the heterogeneous expert routing\n1581\tautomatically adapts to different acoustic contexts without per-scene tuning or explicit scene labels.\n1582\tThis consistent cross-scene performance complements the SNR-stratified analysis in Figure 3d of the\n1583\tmain paper, together demonstrating robust enhancement across both noise type and noise level.\n1584\t\n1585\tE\n1586\t\n1587\tQualitative Visualization\n1588\t\n1589\tFigure 6 shows representative spectrograms before and after enhancement. The denoised output\n1590\texhibits clearer harmonic structures, sharper formant contours, and significantly attenuated broadband\n1591\tnoise, consistent with the objective gains reported in Table 1. Note that some high-frequency\n1592\t20\n1593\t\n1594\t\fFigure 6: Spectrogram comparison before and after enhancement. HybridSB-MoE attenuates\n1595\tbroadband noise while preserving speech harmonics and formant structure. Color bar (dB) is shown\n1596\ton the right.\n1597\tcomponents in the enhanced spectrogram appear more pronounced than in the noisy input; this is\n1598\tbecause heavy broadband noise in the input masks underlying speech harmonics that become visible\n1599\tonly after suppression—it reflects accurate harmonic recovery, not bandwidth extension or content\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e71df7e2-cb51-4e95-971f-0cc9eee0f3dc", "uuid": "9b07a55c-7a95-4d6c-a6a0-3e44a20fb967", "timestamp": "2026-08-14T06:11:48.556Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "20:\nLSB ← Ldata\nSB + λpath Lpath + λtraj Ltraj\n21:\nL ← Lrec + λSB LSB + λaux Laux + λcal Lcal\n22:\nUpdate Θ ← Θ − η∇Θ L\n23: end for\n\nC\n\nProof of Theorem 1\n\nWe prove the K-step discretization bound stated in Section 3 (Theorem 1) of the main paper. The\nstrategy is to decompose the W2 distance into (i) a one-step model-fidelity error driven by L⋆path and\nL⋆traj , and (ii) a Riemann-sum discretization error in K controlled by the schedule’s front-loading\nexponent γ, then to combine the two via a synchronous coupling and a triangle inequality. The\nargument adapts standard SDE-discretization analysis (cf. [37, 26]) to the doubly-conditioned bridge\nprocess of Eq. (4–5).\nC.1\n\nSetup and Notation\n\nFix a noisy observation y and let {xt }t∈[0,T ] be the true continuous-time bridge process from\nclean speech x to y, so that pbr\nt (· | y) is its time-t marginal. Let x̂θ be the trained denoiser,\nand let p̂K be the law at t=0 of the K-step rollout produced by Eq. (5) initialized at xT = y\nγ\nalong the non-uniform schedule\n{tk }K\nk=0 with tK = T and tk = T (k/K) , γ ∈ (0, 1]. We use\nR\n2\n2\nW2 (µ, ν) = inf π∈Γ(µ,ν) ∥u − v∥ dπ(u, v) in the standard definition.\nThroughout, C denotes a generic constant depending only on the regularity constants of Assumption\n1; its value may change line by line.\n17\n\n\fC.2\n\nOne-step Model-fidelity Lemma\n\nLemma 1 (Model-fidelity error). Under Assumption 1, for each step tk → tk−1 of Eq. (5), the\nconditional one-step error\nh\ni\n2\nδk := E x̂θ (xtk , y, tk ) − x\nxtk , y\nsatisfies E[δk ] ≤ C (L⋆path + L⋆traj ) uniformly in k, where the expectation on the right is over the\nbridge construction Eq. (4).\nProof. The trajectory regularizer Ltraj of Eq. (8), evaluated at the optimum, satisfies\n\u0015\n\u0014\nq\nq\n2\n= L⋆traj .\nE xt − ( β̄t x̂θ (xt , y, t) + 1 − β̄t y)\np\np\nSubstituting the forward construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ from Eq. (4) and rearranging,\nq\n\u0002\n\u0003\nβ̄t E ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 − 2 β̄t σt E[⟨x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x, ϵ⟩] + σt2 E∥ϵ∥2 = L⋆traj .\nBy\nand E∥ϵ∥2 = d (the signal dimension), the cross term is bounded by\np\n√ Cauchy–Schwarz\n2\n2 dσt E∥x̂θ − x∥ , and bounded marginals (Assumption 1(iii)) yield\n\u0002\n\u0003\n\u0001\n1 ⋆\n2\nE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x∥2 ≤\nLtraj + Cσt2 ≤ C(L⋆traj + σmax\n),\nβ̄t\n\n(13)\n\non [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] for any fixed ϵ0 > 0, since β̄t is bounded away from zero on this set; near the\nboundaries, the schedule’s vanishing σt controls the residual.\nThe path-consistency loss Lpath of Eq. (7) controls the deviation of x̂θ across timesteps:\n\u0002\n\u0003\nE ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t) − x̂θ (xt′ , y, t′ )∥2 ≤ 2L⋆path .\n\n(14)\n\nCombining Eq. (13) with Eq. (14) and the triangle inequality on the implied error gives the claimed\nbound on E[δk ].\nC.3\n\nDiscretization Lemma\n\np\np\nLemma 2 (Riemann discretization rate). Let Φ(t) = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y denote the deterministic\ncomponent of the bridge state at time t (Eq. 4 with σt ϵ removed). Under Assumption 1, the K-step\nRiemann approximation ΦK of Φ along the non-uniform schedule tk = T (k/K)γ , γ ∈ (0, 1],\nsatisfies\nsup Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 ) ≤ C K − min(1,γ) .\nk\n\nProof. Standard mean-value estimates on C 1 schedules yield local error |Φ(tk−1 ) − ΦK (tk−1 )| ≤\nC (∆tk ) ∥Φ′ ∥∞ on [tk−1 , tk ], where ∆tk = tk − tk−1 . For the schedule tk = T (k/K)γ ,\n\u0001\n∆tk = T (k/K)γ − ((k − 1)/K)γ ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 .\nFor γ ≤ 1, k γ−1 is non-increasing in k, so supk ∆tk = ∆t1 ≤ T K −γ , giving the stated bound with\nexponent min(1, γ) = γ.\nRemark. Front-loading (γ<1) does not improve the worst-case rate exponent. However, since\n∆tk ≤ T γK −γ k γ−1 is non-increasing in k for γ ≤ 1, the schedule concentrates many small\nsteps near tK = T , where the reverse rollout of Eq. 5 is initialized at xT = y and where per-step\nerrors propagate through all subsequent reverse-sampling iterations. The single large step ∆t1 , by\ncontrast, traverses the low-t regime in which xt already lies close to x and x̂θ is most accurate.\nThis redistribution—not the asymptotic rate—is what empirically dominates small-K reconstruction\nquality.\n18\n\n\fC.4\n\nCombining the Two Sources of Error\n\nLet x̂(K) denote the random variable whose law is p̂K , and let X ∼ pbr\n0 . We construct a synchronous\ncoupling between x̂(K) and X by sharing the noise variables z across the K rollout steps with the\ncorresponding bridge perturbation ϵ in the continuous process. Under this coupling,\nK\nK\nh\ni\nX\nY\nX\n\u00012\n(K)\n2\n2\nW22 (p̂K , pbr\n)\n≤\nE\n∥x̂\n−\nX∥\n≤\n2\n∆t\nE[δ\n]\n·\n(1\n+\nL\n∆t\n)\n+\n2\n∆tk · disck ,\nk\nk\nx\nj\n0\nk=1\n\nj<k\n\nk=1\n\nwhere disck is the Riemann error from Lemma 2; the ∆tk weighting on the model-fidelity term tracks\nthe time-averaged definition of L⋆traj , L⋆path (Eqs. 8,7), so each step contributes proportionally to its\nQ\ntime interval. The Lipschitz factor j (1 + Lx ∆tj ) is uniformly bounded by exp(Lx T ), absorbing it\nP\ninto the constant. By Lemma 1 and k ∆tk = T , the first sum is bounded by C T (L⋆path + L⋆traj ),\nP\nindependent of K. By Lemma 2 together with k (∆tk )2 ≤ T supk ∆tk = T 2 K −γ ,√the second\n√\n√\nsum is bounded by C K −2 min(1,γ) . Taking square roots and applying a + b ≤ a + b yields\nq\n−α\nW2 (p̂K , pbr\n+ C2 L⋆path + L⋆traj ,\n0 ) ≤ C1 K\nwhich is exactly Eq. (9).\nC.5\n\nJustification of Assumption 1\n\nWe address each of the three regularity conditions in turn and explain why each is reasonable in our\nsetting.\n(i) Lipschitz continuity of x̂θ . The condition that x̂θ (·, y, t) is Lx -Lipschitz in its first argument and\nLt -Lipschitz in t is standard in diffusion-sampling analyses [37, 26]. It is justified architecturally\nand empirically: (a) our denoiser is a 1D U-Net with GroupNorm [47] and SiLU activations, both\nLipschitz; (b) the convolutional layers’ Lipschitz constants are bounded since weights remain finite\nunder our AdamW training with weight decay 0.01 and gradient clipping at norm 1.0 (Appendix B);\n(c) timestep dependence enters only through a smooth sinusoidal embedding followed by FiLM\nconditioning, which is C ∞ in t and so trivially Lipschitz on the compact interval [0, T ]. We do\nnot enforce a target Lx explicitly (e.g., via spectral normalization); the assumption asserts that the\ntrained network is Lipschitz with some finite constant, which is automatic for any network without\nunbounded activations or singular layers. Tighter constant estimates—needed only for quantitatively\ntight versions of the bound—would require spectral norm tracking and are left to future work.\n(ii) Smoothness of the schedules. The cosine schedule β̄t = f (t)/f (0) with f (t) = cos2 ((t/T +\ns)/(1 + s) · π/2) is in fact C ∞ on [0, T ], considerably stronger than the C 1 requirement: it is the\ncomposition of a smooth trigonometric function with an affine reparameterization. Its monotonicity\nfollows from cos2 being strictly decreasing on the relevant subinterval, and its first and second\nderivatives are uniformly bounded on [0, T ] since the schedule arguments stay in a compact\np subset of\n[0, π/2] where cos and sin are bounded. The bridge-perturbation magnitude σt = σmax β̄t (1 − β̄t )\nis C ∞ on the open interval (0, T ) and continuous on [0, T ] with σ0 = σT = 0; the square-root\nintroduces a one-sided Hölder singularity exactly at the endpoints, but standard SDE-discretization\narguments (e.g., [37]) accommodate this via a small endpoint truncation [ϵ0 , T − ϵ0 ] that vanishes in\nthe bound’s leading order. In our setting all sampling steps lie strictly in the interior of [0, T ] with\ntK = T at the noisy endpoint where the bridge is deterministic, so endpoint singularities do not affect\nthe discretization.\n(iii) Bounded second moments.\nE∥xt ∥2 < ∞ uniformly in t follows directly from\np The condition\np\nthe bridge construction xt = β̄t x + 1 − β̄t y + σt ϵ: by Jensen’s inequality and the triangle\ninequality,\n2\nE∥xt ∥2 ≤ 3E∥x∥2 + 3E∥y∥2 + 3σmax\nd,\nwhere d is the signal dimension and the right-hand side is finite because audio waveforms are bounded\n(∥x∥∞ , ∥y∥∞ ≤ 1 after our standard amplitude normalization, so E∥x∥2 ≤ d and similarly for y).\nFor E∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥2 , the Lipschitz condition (i) gives ∥x̂θ (xt , y, t)∥ ≤ Lx ∥xt ∥ + |x̂θ (0, y, t)|, so\nfiniteness of the second moment of x̂θ follows from finiteness of E∥xt ∥2 together with boundedness\nof x̂θ at the reference input xt = 0 (a finite-norm constant for any fixed trained network on a fixed\n19\n\n\fPESQ\n\nSTOI (%)\n\nDomestic\n\nNatural\n\nOffice\n\nPublic\n\nTransportation\n\n96.6\n3.92\n\nPESQ\n\n95.8\n\n3.84\n\n3.80\n\nSTOI (%)\n\n96.2\n\n3.88\n\nPublic\n\nUS\nTB\n\nTC\nAR\n\nO\nET\nR\nTM\n\nER\nET\nPC\nAF\n\nN\n\nES\nTO\nPR\n\nG\n\nOffice\n\nPS\nTAT\nIO\n\nTIN\nEE\n\nICE\n\nNatural\n\nOM\n\nFF\nOO\n\nNF\n\nIEL\n\nD\n\nK\nNP\nAR\n\nIVE\nR\n\nN\n\nDomestic\n\nNR\n\nITC\nHE\nDK\n\nNG\nIVI\nDL\n\nDW\nAS\nHIN\n\nG\n\n95.4\n\nTransportation\n\nFigure 5: Scene-stratified performance across all 14 noise types in VoiceBank+DEMAND. Inner\nring: PESQ; outer ring: STOI (%). Colors indicate scene categories: Domestic (blue), Nature (green),\nOffice (orange), Public (red), Transport (purple). For reference, the strongest baseline ROSE-CD\n[33] achieves a mean PESQ of 3.85 across these conditions; our method exceeds this in every scene,\nwith PESQ ranging from 3.84 to 3.92 (mean 3.88, standard deviation <0.03 across scenes). Y-axis\nranges are zoomed for visibility of cross-scene variation.\nobservation y). Both moments are therefore bounded by a constant depending only on Lx , σmax , d,\nand the amplitude bound on the data.\nIn summary, Assumption 1 encodes mild structural properties that hold automatically for our cosinescheduled SB construction with a standard U-Net denoiser trained on amplitude-normalized waveforms. The assumption fails only in pathological regimes (unbounded data, divergent network outputs,\nor non-smooth schedules) that are not relevant to our setting or to standard SE practice.\nC.6\n\nDiscussion\n\nA few caveats deserve mention. First, Assumption 1(i) is a Lipschitz condition on the trained network;\nit can be enforced via spectral normalization in practice, but in our experiments it is assumed rather\nthan explicitly imposed. Second, the bound is presented as a design-justifying inequality; it is loose\nin the sense that the constants C1 , C2 involve worst-case Lipschitz factors and are not numerically\nestimated here. A tighter quantitative match to empirical PESQ-vs-K would require finer Lipschitz\nestimates on the U-Net and is left for future work. Third, the bound shows that small K is justified\nconditional on the regularizers reaching small training-time values; if a model is trained without these\nterms, the second term of Eq. (9) does not vanish and the bound cannot guarantee small-K quality.\nThis is consistent with the role we ascribe to Lpath + Ltraj in the main paper.\n\nD\n\nScene-Stratified Performance\n\nFigure 5 breaks down enhancement performance across all 14 noise environments. Natural environments (NPARK, NRIVER, NFIELD) achieve the highest scores due to relatively stable spectral\ncharacteristics, while public spaces (PCAFETER, PSTATION, PRESTO) are slightly more challenging due to non-stationary crowd noise and unpredictable transient events. Despite this variation, the\nspread is small (PESQ standard deviation <0.03), indicating that the heterogeneous expert routing\nautomatically adapts to different acoustic contexts without per-scene tuning or explicit scene labels.\nThis consistent cross-scene performance complements the SNR-stratified analysis in Figure 3d of the\nmain paper, together demonstrating robust enhancement across both noise type and noise level.\n\nE\n\nQualitative Visualization\n\nFigure 6 shows representative spectrograms before and after enhancement. The denoised output\nexhibits clearer harmonic structures, sharper formant contours, and significantly attenuated broadband\nnoise, consistent with the objective gains reported in Table 1. Note that some high-frequency\n20\n\n\fFigure 6: Spectrogram comparison before and after enhancement. HybridSB-MoE attenuates\nbroadband noise while preserving speech harmonics and formant structure. Color bar (dB) is shown\non the right.\ncomponents in the enhanced spectrogram appear more pronounced than in the noisy input; this is\nbecause heavy broadband noise in the input masks underlying speech harmonics that become visible\nonly after suppression—it reflects accurate harmonic recovery, not bandwidth extension or content", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1200, "totalLines": 1604}}}
